pal visual attribute.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04509","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4510","nodeId":"REVISION85-4510","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The winged lion is the traditional symbol of which Evangelist?","value":"Mark","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetramorph","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Mark is represented by the lion; Matthew by a human figure, Luke by an ox and John by an eagle.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04510","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4511","nodeId":"REVISION85-4511","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"An ointment jar often serves as the attribute of:","value":"Mary Magdalene","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Magdalene","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"The vessel recalls anointing traditions associated with Mary Magdalene in Western iconography.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04511","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4512","nodeId":"REVISION85-4512","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Botticelli's Birth of Venus draws its subject from:","value":"Classical mythology","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Birth_of_Venus","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Venus is the classical goddess of love, presented arriving at the shore.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04512","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4513","nodeId":"REVISION85-4513","anchor":"ENT:TITIAN","subject":"Titian's Bacchus and Ariadne shows an encounter from:","value":"Classical mythology","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/titian-bacchus-and-ariadne","checkedOnline85":true}],"explanation74":"The painting treats the meeting of Bacchus and Ariadne on Naxos.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04513","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4514","nodeId":"REVISION85-4514","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Rodin's Gates of Hell drew its initial literary inspiration from:","value":"Dante's Inferno","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gates_of_Hell","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"The sculptural portal was conceived around the Inferno, though its imagery evolved over time.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04514","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4515","nodeId":"REVISION85-4515","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Brâncuși's Endless Column stands at Târgu Jiu in:","value":"Romania","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"place geography","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endless_Column","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"The column forms part of Brâncuși's memorial ensemble at Târgu Jiu.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04515","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4516","nodeId":"REVISION85-4516","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Klimt's The Kiss is particularly associated with the use of:","value":"Gold leaf","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Kiss_(Klimt)","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"The work belongs to Klimt's Golden Phase and incorporates gold leaf.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04516","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4517","nodeId":"REVISION85-4517","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which artistic tendency uses the physical landscape as both material and site?","value":"Land Art","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_art","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Land artists work with earth, terrain and location, often outside conventional gallery spaces.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04517","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4518","nodeId":"REVISION85-4518","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which artist initiated Kassel's project pairing thousands of planted trees with basalt stones?","value":"Joseph Beuys","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://documenta.de/en/retrospective/documenta-7","checkedOnline85":true}],"explanation74":"Beuys's 7000 Oaks project paired new trees with basalt markers and treated ecological change as art.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04518","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4519","nodeId":"REVISION85-4519","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Christo and Jeanne-Claude are particularly associated with:","value":"Wrapping buildings and landscapes","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christo_and_Jeanne-Claude","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Their temporary projects transformed familiar sites through fabric and large-scale installations.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04519","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4520","nodeId":"REVISION85-4520","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Giuseppe Arcimboldo constructed painted portraits from:","value":"Fruit, vegetables and other objects","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuseppe_Arcimboldo","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Arcimboldo's composite heads combine recognisable objects into human faces.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04520","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4521","nodeId":"REVISION85-4521","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Francesco Hayez's The Kiss is often interpreted in connection with the:","value":"Risorgimento","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Kiss_(Hayez)","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"The painting's romantic imagery is closely associated with the political climate of Italian unification.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04521","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4522","nodeId":"REVISION85-4522","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Giovanni Fattori is associated with which Italian group?","value":"Macchiaioli","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Fattori","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Fattori was a leading Macchiaiolo, using patches of tone and colour to organise scenes.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04522","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4523","nodeId":"REVISION85-4523","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Antonio Canova's The Three Graces depicts figures from:","value":"Classical mythology","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Three_Graces_(sculpture)","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"The Graces are classical personifications associated with beauty and charm.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04523","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4524","nodeId":"REVISION85-4524","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Donatello's wooden Penitent Magdalene emphasises the saint's:","value":"Ascetic suffering","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penitent_Magdalene_(Donatello)","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Its emaciated figure and rough appearance express penitence rather than idealised youthful beauty.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04524","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4525","nodeId":"REVISION85-4525","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Verrocchio's equestrian monument in Venice commemorates:","value":"Bartolomeo Colleoni","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/andrea-del-verrocchio","checkedOnline85":true}],"explanation74":"Verrocchio's bronze monument honours the condottiero Bartolomeo Colleoni.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04525","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4526","nodeId":"REVISION85-4526","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The Riace Bronze warriors were discovered:","value":"In the sea","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://cultura.gov.it/luogo/museo-archeologico-nazionale-di-reggio-calabria-bronzi-di-riace","checkedOnline85":true}],"explanation74":"The statues were found by a diver in the sea off Riace, Calabria, in 1972.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04526","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4527","nodeId":"REVISION85-4527","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The Winged Victory of Samothrace represents the goddess:","value":"Nike","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winged_Victory_of_Samothrace","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Nike personifies victory; the statue is a major Hellenistic sculpture.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04527","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4528","nodeId":"REVISION85-4528","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The Venus de Milo is a work of:","value":"Ancient Greek sculpture","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_de_Milo","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"The marble statue is usually dated to the Hellenistic period.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04528","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4529","nodeId":"REVISION85-4529","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"In drypoint printmaking, lines are made chiefly with a:","value":"Sharp needle","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/ko/essays/the-printed-image-in-the-west-drypoint","checkedOnline85":true}],"explanation74":"A needle scratches directly into the plate, raising a burr that contributes to the printed line.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04529","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4530","nodeId":"REVISION85-4530","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"A tapestry's pictorial design is created chiefly by:","value":"Weaving coloured threads","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tapestry","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"In a tapestry, the image is woven into the textile rather than painted onto it.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04530","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION85-4531","nodeId":"REVISION85-4531","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Marquetry decorates a surface through:","value":"Inlaid pieces of wood veneer","group":"Design","major":"Design","era":"Other","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original general-knowledge question: topic reference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marquetry","checkedOnline85":false}],"explanation74":"Marquetry assembles thin pieces of veneer into decorative patterns, especially on furniture.","questionId85":"GEN43-Q04531","reviewStatus85":"ready"},{"id":"REVISION86-4532","nodeId":"REVISION86-4532","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What was the original purpose of the Fagus Factory?","value":"Shoe-last production","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/uploads/nominations/1368.pdf","checkedOnline86":true,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-651877e64e87","atlasSeedTitle":"Fagus workshops in Alfeld","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[75]}]}],"explanation74":"The Fagus Factory made lasts, the forms used in shoemaking.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-651877e64e87"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04532"},{"id":"REVISION86-4533","nodeId":"REVISION86-4533","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"In Vitruvius's triad, what does utilitas refer to?","value":"Usefulness","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitruvius","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF34-CAND-b351b0691a79","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · firmitas, utilitas and venustas","sourceReferences":[{"file":"Important people from the past year papers By~Kaan Özhan.pdf","pages":[10]}]}],"explanation74":"Utilitas concerns a building's suitability for its intended use.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF34-CAND-b351b0691a79"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04533"},{"id":"REVISION86-4534","nodeId":"REVISION86-4534","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The Basilica Palladiana in Vicenza was principally a building for what purpose?","value":"Civic administration","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_Palladiana","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF34-CAND-8087dedccbb4","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · Basilica Palladiana in Vicenza","sourceReferences":[{"file":"Important people from the past year papers By~Kaan Özhan.pdf","pages":[176]}]}],"explanation74":"Despite its name, the Basilica Palladiana is a civic building, incorporating the city's historic town hall.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF34-CAND-8087dedccbb4"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04534"},{"id":"REVISION86-4535","nodeId":"REVISION86-4535","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The Teatro Olimpico's permanent stage scenery represents the streets of which ancient city?","value":"Thebes","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://www.teatrolimpicovicenza.it/en/the-theatre/introduction","checkedOnline86":true,"atlasSeedId":"PDF34-CAND-35728e73669e","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · Teatro Olimpico in Vicenza","sourceReferences":[{"file":"Important people from the past year papers By~Kaan Özhan.pdf","pages":[177]}]}],"explanation74":"Vincenzo Scamozzi's perspective streets were made for a performance of Oedipus Rex, set in Thebes.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF34-CAND-35728e73669e"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04535"},{"id":"REVISION86-4536","nodeId":"REVISION86-4536","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What was the Palazzina di Caccia di Stupinigi built as?","value":"A hunting lodge","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palazzina_di_caccia_of_Stupinigi","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF34-CAND-5fbfa4cd3214","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · Palazzina di Caccia di Stupinigi","sourceReferences":[{"file":"Important people from the past year papers By~Kaan Özhan.pdf","pages":[198]}]}],"explanation74":"Juvarra designed Stupinigi as a hunting residence for the House of Savoy.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF34-CAND-5fbfa4cd3214"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04536"},{"id":"REVISION86-4537","nodeId":"REVISION86-4537","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which space forms the central feature of Frank Lloyd Wright's Guggenheim Museum in New York?","value":"A spiral ramp","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solomon_R._Guggenheim_Museum","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-c4305166092c","atlasSeedTitle":"Guggenheim Museum a New York","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[74]}]}],"explanation74":"The continuous spiral ramp wraps around the museum's central atrium.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-c4305166092c"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04537"},{"id":"REVISION86-4538","nodeId":"REVISION86-4538","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The Reichstag dome in Berlin incorporates a central cone covered with what?","value":"Mirrors","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://webarchiv.bundestag.de/archive/2007/0206/interakt/infomat/fremdsprachiges_material/downloads/einblickeEn_download.pdf","checkedOnline86":true,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-26290e8ba8a1","atlasSeedTitle":"New seat of parliament at the Reichstag in Berlin","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[85]}]}],"explanation74":"The mirrored cone directs daylight into the parliamentary chamber below.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-26290e8ba8a1"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04538"},{"id":"REVISION86-4539","nodeId":"REVISION86-4539","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which ancient building type is recalled by the porticoes of Villa Rotonda?","value":"A classical temple","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Capra_%22La_Rotonda%22","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PROSPECT-00165","atlasSeedTitle":"Identify a characteristic building element of Villa Rotonda","sourceReferences":[]}],"explanation74":"Palladio used temple-front porticoes on all four sides of the villa.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PROSPECT-00165"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04539"},{"id":"REVISION86-4540","nodeId":"REVISION86-4540","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What is the principal material of the Louvre Pyramid's transparent cladding?","value":"Glass","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louvre_Pyramid","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-8d639efee758","atlasSeedTitle":"Louvre Pyramid","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[79]}]}],"explanation74":"I. M. Pei's pyramid has glass panels supported by a metal structure.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-8d639efee758"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04540"},{"id":"REVISION86-4541","nodeId":"REVISION86-4541","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which monument stands at the centre of Bernini's piazza at St Peter's?","value":"An Egyptian obelisk","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Peter%27s_Square","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF34-CAND-d1dd0ff5bb27","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · Saint Peter's Square in Rome","sourceReferences":[{"file":"Important people from the past year papers By~Kaan Özhan.pdf","pages":[189]}]}],"explanation74":"The Vatican obelisk occupies the centre of St Peter's Square.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF34-CAND-d1dd0ff5bb27"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04541"},{"id":"REVISION86-4542","nodeId":"REVISION86-4542","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The great dome of Florence Cathedral has which plan at its base?","value":"Octagonal","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florence_Cathedral","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF35-CAND-280e031039dfce","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · octagonal dome of Florence Cathedral","sourceReferences":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","pages":[15]}]}],"explanation74":"Brunelleschi's dome rises over an octagonal drum.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF35-CAND-280e031039dfce"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04542"},{"id":"REVISION86-4543","nodeId":"REVISION86-4543","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which building did Gae Aulenti help transform into the Musée d'Orsay?","value":"A railway station","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mus%C3%A9e_d%27Orsay","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF35-CAND-8630c4d71a4ae7","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · conversion of the Musée d'Orsay","sourceReferences":[{"file":"tira miss u.pdf","pages":[55]}]}],"explanation74":"The museum occupies the former Gare d'Orsay; Aulenti led the interior adaptation.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF35-CAND-8630c4d71a4ae7"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04543"},{"id":"REVISION86-4544","nodeId":"REVISION86-4544","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which feature distinguishes the southern facade of the Institut du Monde Arabe in Paris?","value":"Adjustable diaphragm screens","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://www.imarabe.org/fr/magazine/restauration-moucharabiehs-ima-chantier-exception","checkedOnline86":true,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-c2f7db2cab20","atlasSeedTitle":"Jean Nouvel","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[103]}]}],"explanation74":"The geometric metal diaphragms reinterpret mashrabiya screens and were designed to regulate light.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-c2f7db2cab20"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04544"},{"id":"REVISION86-4545","nodeId":"REVISION86-4545","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Shigeru Ban is particularly known for experimenting with which structural component?","value":"Paper tubes","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shigeru_Ban","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-625302c5ef20","atlasSeedTitle":"Shigeru Ban Architects","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[65]}]}],"explanation74":"Ban has used strong paper tubes in emergency shelters and other structures.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-625302c5ef20"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04545"},{"id":"REVISION86-4546","nodeId":"REVISION86-4546","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Titian's Bacchus and Ariadne depicts Ariadne after she has been abandoned by whom?","value":"Theseus","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacchus_and_Ariadne","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-a9bfac981e3c","atlasSeedTitle":"Bacchus and Ariadne","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[8]}]}],"explanation74":"Theseus abandons Ariadne on Naxos, where Bacchus encounters her.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-a9bfac981e3c"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04546"},{"id":"REVISION86-4547","nodeId":"REVISION86-4547","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The figure of Holofernes in paintings by Artemisia Gentileschi is traditionally identified as what?","value":"An enemy general","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Slaying_Holofernes_(Artemisia_Gentileschi%2C_Naples)","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF35-CAND-50d3520253382b","atlasSeedTitle":"Context · Judith Slaying Holofernes","sourceReferences":[{"file":"tira miss u.pdf","pages":[29]}]}],"explanation74":"In the Book of Judith, Holofernes is the general whom Judith kills to save her people.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF35-CAND-50d3520253382b"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04547"},{"id":"REVISION86-4548","nodeId":"REVISION86-4548","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"What are the central figures doing in Matisse's Dance?","value":"Holding hands in a circle","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dance_(Matisse)","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-dcaf8f6ab62a","atlasSeedTitle":"The Dance","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[22]}]}],"explanation74":"Five figures join hands in a circular dance, reduced to bold colour and rhythmic outlines.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-dcaf8f6ab62a"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04548"},{"id":"REVISION86-4549","nodeId":"REVISION86-4549","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The word Quattrocento refers to which period of Italian art?","value":"The fifteenth century","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","relation":"classification style","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quattrocento","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-546ef1ff7808","atlasSeedTitle":"Quattrocento painting","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[4]}]}],"explanation74":"Quattrocento denotes the 1400s, a central period of the Italian Renaissance.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-546ef1ff7808"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04549"},{"id":"REVISION86-4550","nodeId":"REVISION86-4550","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which animal appears beside the sleeping woman in Rousseau's The Sleeping Gypsy?","value":"A lion","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sleeping_Gypsy","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"M77-MOMA-80172","atlasSeedTitle":"The Sleeping Gypsy","sourceReferences":[]