pace beneath a theatre's stage used for machinery?","value":"Understage","group":"Architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stage_machinery","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDF-TOPIC-eedec5cc952d","atlasSeedTitle":"The theatre","sourceReferences":[{"file":"History + Arch Part (1).pdf","pages":[40]}]}],"explanation74":"The understage accommodates equipment below the stage floor.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDF-TOPIC-eedec5cc952d"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04576"},{"id":"REVISION86-4577","nodeId":"REVISION86-4577","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which mountain range separates much of France from Spain?","value":"The Pyrenees","group":"Geography & Civics","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"place geography","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrenees","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"EXP77-1471","atlasSeedTitle":"The Treaty of the Pyrenees settled a conflict between which two monarchies? · context relation","sourceReferences":[]}],"explanation74":"The Pyrenees form the main mountain barrier along the Franco-Spanish border.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["EXP77-1471"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04577"},{"id":"REVISION86-4578","nodeId":"REVISION86-4578","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which instrument measures atmospheric pressure?","value":"Barometer","group":"Science & Tech","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barometer","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"REVISION82-4195","atlasSeedTitle":"Which experimental device is associated with Evangelista Torricelli? · context relation","sourceReferences":[]}],"explanation74":"A barometer measures air pressure; Torricelli developed the mercury barometer.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["REVISION82-4195"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04578"},{"id":"REVISION86-4579","nodeId":"REVISION86-4579","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Diamond is a crystalline form of which element?","value":"Carbon","group":"Science & Tech","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","atlasSeedTitle":"Science & Tech","sourceReferences":[]}],"explanation74":"Diamond is a crystalline form of carbon.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04579"},{"id":"REVISION86-4580","nodeId":"REVISION86-4580","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which term describes a portrait showing the face from one side?","value":"A profile portrait","group":"Art History","major":"Art History","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"PDFART48-15","atlasSeedTitle":"Diptych of the Dukes of Urbino","sourceReferences":[]}],"explanation74":"In a profile portrait, the face is seen from one side.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["PDFART48-15"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04580"},{"id":"REVISION86-4581","nodeId":"REVISION86-4581","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which genre is represented by Aesop's short animal stories?","value":"Fable","group":"Language & Literature","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original question from an Atlas topic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aesop%27s_Fables","checkedOnline86":false,"atlasSeedId":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","atlasSeedTitle":"Language & Literature","sourceReferences":[]}],"explanation74":"A fable commonly uses animals to convey a moral lesson.","sourceAtlasNodeIds":["MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE"],"questionId86":"GEN43-Q04581"},{"id":"REVISION93-4582","nodeId":"REVISION93-4582","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Who wrote Fontamara?","value":"Ignazio Silone","group":"Language & Literature","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"creator work","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/ignazio-silone/","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"Silone’s novel follows an impoverished rural community in Abruzzo under Fascism.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04582"},{"id":"REVISION93-4583","nodeId":"REVISION93-4583","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Cinecittà, the film-studio complex associated with Fellini, is in:","value":"Rome","group":"Language & Literature","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"place context","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.turismoroma.it/en/places/cinecitt%C3%A0-studios","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"Cinecittà opened in Rome in 1937 and became a major centre of Italian film production.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04583"},{"id":"REVISION93-4584","nodeId":"REVISION93-4584","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"The Golden Records carrying sounds and images of Earth travelled aboard which spacecraft?","value":"Voyager 1 and 2","group":"Science & Tech","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/golden-record-contents/","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"The two Voyagers launched in 1977 carrying phonograph records intended as messages about life on Earth.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04584"},{"id":"REVISION93-4585","nodeId":"REVISION93-4585","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Rita Levi-Montalcini’s Nobel-winning research concerned:","value":"Growth factors","group":"Science & Tech","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1986/press-release/","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"Levi-Montalcini identified nerve growth factor; she shared the 1986 medicine prize with Stanley Cohen.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04585"},{"id":"REVISION93-4586","nodeId":"REVISION93-4586","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which combination describes the three dimensions of the Human Development Index?","value":"Health, education, living standards","group":"History & Politics","major":"History & Politics","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://hdr.undp.org/data-center/human-development-index","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"The UNDP index combines measures of longevity, education and income rather than treating income alone as development.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04586"},{"id":"REVISION93-4587","nodeId":"REVISION93-4587","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Sinkholes and underground drainage in limestone are characteristic of:","value":"Karst","group":"Geography & Civics","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-a-sinkhole","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"Water dissolves soluble rock, creating cavities and underground drainage; collapse can produce sinkholes.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04587"},{"id":"REVISION93-4588","nodeId":"REVISION93-4588","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"On a conventional Mercator world map, the greatest exaggeration of area occurs near:","value":"The poles","group":"Geography & Civics","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/how-are-different-map-projections-used","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"Mercator preserves local angles but greatly enlarges areas at high latitudes. Greenland consequently appears disproportionately large.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04588"},{"id":"REVISION93-4589","nodeId":"REVISION93-4589","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which wind brings hot air from North Africa towards the Mediterranean?","value":"Sirocco","group":"Geography & Civics","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/types-of-weather/wind/wind-names","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"The sirocco originates over North Africa and can acquire moisture while crossing the Mediterranean.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04589"},{"id":"REVISION93-4590","nodeId":"REVISION93-4590","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"A fumarole is an opening that emits:","value":"Steam and volcanic gases","group":"Geography & Civics","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/volc/geysers.html","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"Fumaroles release steam and gases from hot volcanic systems; they need not emit lava.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04590"},{"id":"REVISION93-4591","nodeId":"REVISION93-4591","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which twentieth-century poet collected his verse under the title Il Canzoniere?","value":"Umberto Saba","group":"Language & Literature","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"creator work","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/umberto-saba/","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"Saba repeatedly enlarged Il Canzoniere. The century specified distinguishes it from Petrarch’s earlier collection of the same title.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04591"},{"id":"REVISION93-4592","nodeId":"REVISION93-4592","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Rossini’s The Barber of Seville is an example of:","value":"Opera buffa","group":"Language & Literature","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.teatroallascala.org/en/season/2022-2023/opera/il-barbiere-di-siviglia.html","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"The Barber of Seville is a comic opera in two acts, with a libretto by Cesare Sterbini.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04592"},{"id":"REVISION93-4593","nodeId":"REVISION93-4593","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did the United States suspend during the Nixon shock?","value":"Dollar convertibility into gold","group":"History & Politics","major":"History & Politics","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Original · general culture extension","verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/external/about/timeline/index.htm","checkedOnline93":true}],"explanation74":"In 1971 Nixon suspended the dollar’s conversion into gold for foreign official holders, undermining the Bretton Woods monetary system.","questionId93":"GEN43-Q04593"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-001","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-001","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Roman aqueducts normally carried water by:","value":"Gravity","group":"Pont du Gard","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Pont du Gard (Roman Aqueduct)","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/344/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A01","title":"UNESCO — Pont du Gard (Roman Aqueduct)","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/344/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"A slight downward gradient lets gravity move the water. Bridges keep the channel at the required elevation.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-003","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-003","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What problem does a pendentive solve in a domed building?","value":"Supporting a circular dome above a square space","group":"Pendentive","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Smarthistory — Hagia Sophia, Istanbul","verificationUrl":"https://smarthistory.org/hagia-sophia-istanbul/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A03","title":"Smarthistory — Hagia Sophia, Istanbul","url":"https://smarthistory.org/hagia-sophia-istanbul/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"A pendentive is a curved, triangular transition between a square bay and the circular base of a dome. It is not the small opening at the dome’s crown.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-004","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-004","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which feature distinguishes a traditional Doric frieze?","value":"Alternating triglyphs and metopes","group":"Doric frieze","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Architecture in Ancient Greece","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/grarc/hd_grarc.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A04","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Architecture in Ancient Greece","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/grarc/hd_grarc.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Doric frieze alternates grooved triglyphs with metopes. A continuous sculptural frieze is associated with the Ionic order rather than this Doric arrangement.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-008","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-008","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The Crystal Palace of 1851 was chiefly constructed from:","value":"Iron and glass","group":"Crystal Palace","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Victoria and Albert Museum — The Great Exhibition","verificationUrl":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/the-great-exhibition","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A08","title":"Victoria and Albert Museum — The Great Exhibition","url":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/the-great-exhibition","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Paxton used repeated prefabricated components, enabling rapid assembly for the Great Exhibition.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-011","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-011","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"In Le Corbusier’s architecture, what are pilotis?","value":"Columns that lift a building above ground level","group":"Pilotis","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — The Architectural Work of Le Corbusier","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1321/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A11","title":"UNESCO — The Architectural Work of Le Corbusier","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1321/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}},{"file":"Fondation Le Corbusier — Architectural work","verificationUrl":"https://www.fondationlecorbusier.fr/en/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A12","title":"Fondation Le Corbusier — Architectural work","url":"https://www.fondationlecorbusier.fr/en/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Pilotis replace continuous ground-floor supporting walls with columns. They can free the ground beneath a building for circulation or other uses.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-012","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-012","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Le Corbusier’s Unité d’Habitation in Marseille combines apartments with:","value":"Communal services","group":"Unité d’Habitation","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — The Architectural Work of Le Corbusier","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1321/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A11","title":"UNESCO — The Architectural Work of Le Corbusier","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1321/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}},{"file":"Fondation Le Corbusier — Architectural work","verificationUrl":"https://www.fondationlecorbusier.fr/en/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A12","title":"Fondation Le Corbusier — Architectural work","url":"https://www.fondationlecorbusier.fr/en/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The housing block incorporates shared facilities, including shops and rooftop amenities.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-013","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-013","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Fallingwater is an example of Wright’s concept of:","value":"Organic architecture","group":"Fallingwater","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Fallingwater — Architecture","verificationUrl":"https://fallingwater.org/history/about-fallingwater/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A13","title":"Fallingwater — Architecture","url":"https://fallingwater.org/history/about-fallingwater/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The house’s terraces and materials connect it to the rock ledges and stream of its site.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-015","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-015","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which capital was purpose-built inland in the twentieth century as part of a national development project?","value":"Brasília","group":"Brasilia","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Brasilia","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/445/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A15","title":"UNESCO — Brasilia","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/445/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Brasília became Brazil’s capital in 1960. Its planned development moved the national capital away from the coast and created a major experiment in modern urban design.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-017","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-017","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What is a trullo, as found in Alberobello?","value":"A dry-stone dwelling with a characteristic conical roof","group":"Trulli","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — The Trulli of Alberobello","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/787/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A17","title":"UNESCO — The Trulli of Alberobello","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/787/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Trulli belong to a local building tradition in southern Italy. Their conical stone roofs are part of a construction system, not simply decorative towers added to ordinary houses.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-021","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-021","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"A peripteral Greek temple has which arrangement?","value":"A single colonnade running around all sides of the cella","group":"Peripteral temple","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Architecture in Ancient Greece","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/grarc/hd_grarc.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A04","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Architecture in Ancient Greece","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/grarc/hd_grarc.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"“Peripteral” describes the surrounding colonnade. It is a plan arrangement, whereas Doric and Ionic identify architectural orders.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-022","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-022","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which arch form is most characteristic of mature Gothic architecture?","value":"Pointed arch","group":"Romanesque and Gothic","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Romanesque Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/rmsq/hd_rmsq.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A21","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Romanesque Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/rmsq/hd_rmsq.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}},{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Gothic Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/mgot/hd_mgot.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A02","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Gothic Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/mgot/hd_mgot.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Pointed arches are central to Gothic construction, together with rib vaults and buttressing.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-023","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-023","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Seurat’s A Sunday on La Grande Jatte is associated with:","value":"Pointillism","group":"Seurat and optical colour","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Art Institute of Chicago — A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884","verificationUrl":"https://www.artic.edu/artworks/27992/a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P01","title":"Art Institute of Chicago — A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884","url":"https://www.artic.edu/artworks/27992/a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Separate touches of colour form part of Seurat’s systematic approach to optical colour effects.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-026","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-026","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Hokusai’s famous Great Wave was produced using which medium?","value":"Polychrome woodblock printing","group":"The Great Wave","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Under the Wave off Kanagawa","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/45434","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P04","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Under the Wave off Kanagawa","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/45434","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The image belongs to the Japanese ukiyo-e print tradition. Printing from carved blocks allowed multiple impressions, unlike a single painted canvas.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-027","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-027","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The Scream by Edvard Munch is most closely associated with:","value":"Expressionism","group":"The Scream","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Museum of Norway — The Scream","verificationUrl":"https://www.nasjonalmuseet.no/en/collection/object/NG.M.00939","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P05","title":"National Museum of Norway — The Scream","url":"https://www.nasjonalmuseet.no/en/collection/object/NG.M.00939","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Distorted form and intense colour convey psychological anxiety rather than detached observation.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-029","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-029","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"What does sfumato do to painted contours?","value":"Softens transitions so that outlines are not sharply defined","group":"Sfumato","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Gallery — Sfumato","verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/sfumato","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P07","title":"National Gallery — Sfumato","url":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/sfumato","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Sfumato creates gradual, smoky transitions between tones. It concerns the handling of edges and shading, not simply the use of a dark background.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-031","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-031","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which approach is characteristic of Cubism?","value":"Fragmenting forms and combining different viewpoints","group":"Cubism","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Cubism","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/cube/hd_cube.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P09","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Cubism","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/cube/hd_cube.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Cubist artists questioned the single fixed viewpoint of traditional perspective. Objects can be broken into planes and reorganized within the picture.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-032","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-032","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which source of imagery was especially important to Surrealist artists?","value":"Dreams and the unconscious","group":"Surrealism","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Surrealism","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/surr/hd_surr.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P10","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Surrealism","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/surr/hd_surr.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Surrealism explored mental associations beyond ordinary rational control. Dream-like combinations may therefore appear even when individual objects are painted realistically.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-034","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-034","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which concern is closely associated with Impressionist painting?","value":"Changing effects of light in modern life and outdoor scenes","group":"Impressionism","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Impressionism","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/imml/hd_imml.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P12","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Impressionism","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/imml/hd_imml.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Impressionist painters frequently explored transient light and contemporary experience. Visible brushwork helps record perception rather than conceal the process of painting.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-036","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-036","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which combination most closely describes a common Baroque pictorial effect?","value":"Dramatic light, movement and emotionally charged action","group":"Baroque painting","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Gallery — Baroque","verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/baroque","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P14","title":"National Gallery — Baroque","url":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/baroque","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Many Baroque painters sought immediacy through strong contrasts and active compositions. This is a broad tendency, not a rule that every Baroque painting must look identical.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-037","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-037","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"In bronze casting, the form of the model is reproduced using a:","value":"Mould","group":"Bronze casting","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Victoria and Albert Museum — Bronze casting","verificationUrl":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/bronze-casting","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P15","title":"Victoria and Albert Museum — Bronze casting","url":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/bronze-casting","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"A mould receives molten metal; casting permits more than one version of a model.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-038","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-038","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"What does iconography mainly study?","value":"Subjects, attributes and symbols represented in images","group":"Iconography","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Gallery — Iconography","verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/iconography","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"P16","title":"National Gallery — Iconography","url":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/iconography","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Iconography asks what an image represents and how it can be recognized. A key, halo or particular gesture can help identify a figure or subject.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-046","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-046","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Luther’s Ninety-Five Theses chiefly challenged the practice surrounding:","value":"Indulgences","group":"Luther’s Ninety-Five Theses","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Fordham University — Luther’s Ninety-Five Theses","verificationUrl":"https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/luther95.txt","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H08","title":"Fordham University — Luther’s Ninety-Five Theses","url":"https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/luther95.txt","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Luther disputed claims about indulgences and the remission of punishment for sin.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-047","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-047","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"In the Atlantic slave trade, what was the Middle Passage?","value":"The forced sea journey of enslaved Africans to the Americas","group":"Middle Passage","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Royal Museums Greenwich — The Middle Passage","verificationUrl":"https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/topics/what-was-middle-passage","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H09","title":"Royal Museums Greenwich — The Middle Passage","url":"https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/topics/what-was-middle-passage","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The term refers to a coercive transatlantic journey endured by enslaved people, not voluntary labour migration. It formed part of a larger Atlantic trading system.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-051","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-051","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"A central aim of the Congress of Vienna was the restoration of:","value":"A balance of power","group":"Congress of Vienna","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UK National Archives — Congress of Vienna","verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/congress-vienna/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H12","title":"UK National Archives — Congress of Vienna","url":"https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/congress-vienna/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The settlement sought a stable European order after the Napoleonic Wars.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-052","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-052","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What historical process does the term Risorgimento usually describe?","value":"The movement towards Italian national unification","group":"Risorgimento","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Museo Nazionale del Risorgimento Italiano","verificationUrl":"https://www.museorisorgimentotorino.it/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H13","title":"Museo Nazionale del Risorgimento Italiano","url":"https://www.museorisorgimentotorino.it/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Risorgimento includes political, military and cultural movements connected with the formation of a unified Italian state. It is distinct from the Renaissance.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-053","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-053","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which concept is central to Marx and Engels’ Communist Manifesto?","value":"Class struggle","group":"Communist Manifesto","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Manifesto of the Communist Party and Das Kapital","verificationUrl":"https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/manifesto-communist-party-and-das-kapital","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H14","title":"UNESCO — Manifesto of the Communist Party and Das Kapital","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/manifesto-communist-party-and-das-kapital","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The manifesto interprets social and political history through conflicts between classes.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-054","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-054","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which event triggered the July Crisis of 1914?","value":"The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo","group":"Outbreak of the First World War","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Imperial War Museums — How the World Went to War in 1914","verificationUrl":"https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/how-the-world-went-to-war-in-1914","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H15","title":"Imperial War Museums — How the World Went to War in 1914","url":"https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/how-the-world-went-to-war-in-1914","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The assassination set off a diplomatic and military crisis. It was a trigger within a wider situation involving alliances, rivalries and mobilization, not a complete explanation by itself.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-055","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-055","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Treaty of Versailles of 1919 was principally a peace settlement with which country?","value":"Germany","group":"Treaty of Versailles","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Imperial War Museums — The Peace Treaties That Ended the First World War","verificationUrl":"https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/the-peace-treaties-that-ended-the-first-world-war","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H16","title":"Imperial War Museums — The Peace Treaties That Ended the First World War","url":"https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/the-peace-treaties-that-ended-the-first-world-war","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Versailles was the main Allied peace treaty with Germany after the First World War. Other defeated states were dealt with in separate treaties.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-056","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-056","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The League of Nations was founded on the principle of:","value":"Collective security","group":"League of Nations","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"United Nations — League of Nations","verificationUrl":"https://www.ungeneva.org/en/about/league-of-nations","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H17","title":"United Nations — League of Nations","url":"https://www.ungeneva.org/en/about/league-of-nations","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Members were to act collectively against threats to peace.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-057","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-057","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which leader was central to the Bolshevik seizure of power in 1917?","value":"Vladimir Lenin","group":"Bolsheviks","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Imperial War Museums — What Was the Russian Revolution?","verificationUrl":"https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/what-was-the-russian-revolution","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"H18","title":"Imperial War Museums — What Was the Russian Revolution?","url":"https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/what-was-the-russian-revolution","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Lenin led the Bolsheviks during the Russian Revolution. He should not be confused with Stalin, who consolidated power in the Soviet Union later.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-059","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-059","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which United Nations body has primary responsibility for international peace and security?","value":"The Security Council","group":"UN Security Council","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"United Nations Security Council — What Is the Security Council?","verificationUrl":"https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/about_test1","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C01","title":"United Nations Security Council — What Is the Security Council?","url":"https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/about_test1","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Different UN bodies have different mandates. The Security Council’s responsibility for peace and security is distinct from health, education or labour programmes.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-061","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-061","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which example best fits UNESCO’s concept of intangible cultural heritage?","value":"A traditional craft practice passed between generations","group":"Intangible heritage","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — What is Intangible Cultural Heritage?","verificationUrl":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/what-is-intangible-heritage-00003","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C03","title":"UNESCO — What is Intangible Cultural Heritage?","url":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/what-is-intangible-heritage-00003","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Intangible heritage concerns living practices, expressions, knowledge and skills. Its transmission is central; it is not limited to monuments or museum objects.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-064","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-064","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What is a referendum?","value":"A direct vote by the electorate on a specific question","group":"Referendum","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UK Parliament — Referendum","verificationUrl":"https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/referendum/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C06","title":"UK Parliament — Referendum","url":"https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/referendum/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"A referendum asks eligible voters to decide a stated issue directly. It differs from choosing representatives in a general election and from conducting an opinion poll.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-068","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-068","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which person most clearly meets the standard statistical idea of unemployment?","value":"Someone without work who is available and actively seeking a job","group":"Unemployment","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"International Labour Organization — Unemployment rate","verificationUrl":"https://ilostat.ilo.org/resources/concepts-and-definitions/description-unemployment-rate/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C10","title":"International Labour Organization — Unemployment rate","url":"https://ilostat.ilo.org/resources/concepts-and-definitions/description-unemployment-rate/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Unemployment is not simply the absence of a job. Standard measures distinguish unemployment from being outside the labour force.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-069","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-069","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"A country has a trade deficit when, over the relevant period, what is true?","value":"The value of imports exceeds the value of exports","group":"Trade deficit","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"US Bureau of Economic Analysis — International trade","verificationUrl":"https://www.bea.gov/resources/learning-center/what-to-know-international-trade","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C11","title":"US Bureau of Economic Analysis — International trade","url":"https://www.bea.gov/resources/learning-center/what-to-know-international-trade","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"A trade deficit describes a balance between imports and exports. It is not the same measure as a government budget deficit or public debt.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-070","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-070","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What is the opportunity cost of choosing one option?","value":"The value of the best alternative that is given up","group":"Opportunity cost","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis — Opportunity Cost","verificationUrl":"https://www.stlouisfed.org/open-vault/2020/january/what-is-opportunity-cost","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C12","title":"Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis — Opportunity Cost","url":"https://www.stlouisfed.org/open-vault/2020/january/what-is-opportunity-cost","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Scarcity means that choosing one use of time or resources can exclude another. Opportunity cost focuses on the next-best forgone alternative, not all alternatives added together.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-071","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-071","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What makes an income tax progressive?","value":"The share of income paid in tax rises as income rises","group":"Progressive taxation","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"US Internal Revenue Service — Understanding Taxes: Progressive Taxes","verificationUrl":"https://apps.irs.gov/app/understandingTaxes/student/whys_thm03_les03.jsp","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C13","title":"US Internal Revenue Service — Understanding Taxes: Progressive Taxes","url":"https://apps.irs.gov/app/understandingTaxes/student/whys_thm03_les03.jsp","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Progressivity concerns the tax burden relative to income. Merely paying a larger cash amount does not by itself establish a progressive structure.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-072","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-072","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Why is street lighting commonly used as an example of a public good?","value":"One person’s use need not reduce others’ use, and excluding passers-by is difficult","group":"Public goods","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"International Monetary Fund — Public Goods","verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/2021/12/Global-public-goods-Chin-basics","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"C14","title":"International Monetary Fund — Public Goods","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/2021/12/Global-public-goods-Chin-basics","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The key ideas are non-rivalry and difficulty of exclusion. A good does not become a public good merely because a government happens to own it.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-074","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-074","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"What long detour can ships often avoid by using the Suez Canal?","value":"Sailing around the southern tip of Africa","group":"Suez Canal route","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Suez Canal Authority — About Suez Canal","verificationUrl":"https://www.suezcanal.gov.eg/English/About/SuezCanal/Pages/AboutSuezCanal.aspx","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G02","title":"Suez Canal Authority — About Suez Canal","url":"https://www.suezcanal.gov.eg/English/About/SuezCanal/Pages/AboutSuezCanal.aspx","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Suez connects the Mediterranean with the Red Sea, shortening many routes between Europe and Asia. The comparable shortcut through Central America is the Panama Canal.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-075","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-075","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"The Bosphorus passes through which major city?","value":"Istanbul","group":"Bosphorus","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Historic Areas of Istanbul","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/356/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G03","title":"UNESCO — Historic Areas of Istanbul","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/356/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Istanbul occupies both sides of the Bosphorus, between its European and Asian shores. The strait is a geographic feature, not simply a political boundary line.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-076","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-076","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which mountain system forms much of Italy’s northern boundary region?","value":"The Alps","group":"Alps","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Alpine Convention — The Alps","verificationUrl":"https://www.alpconv.org/en/home/topics/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G04","title":"Alpine Convention — The Alps","url":"https://www.alpconv.org/en/home/topics/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Alps connect and divide several central and southern European regions. The Apennines, by contrast, extend along much of the Italian peninsula.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-078","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-078","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"How does a river delta generally form?","value":"Sediment accumulates where a river enters a larger body of water","group":"River delta","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"US Geological Survey — Delta","verificationUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/rivers-streams-and-creeks","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G06","title":"US Geological Survey — Delta","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/rivers-streams-and-creeks","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"As a river slows on entering a sea or lake, it can deposit sediment. Not every river mouth develops a large delta, because waves, tides and sediment supply also matter.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-079","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-079","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"What does latitude measure?","value":"Angular distance north or south of the Equator","group":"Latitude","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"NOAA National Ocean Service — Latitude and Longitude","verificationUrl":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/latitude.html","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G07","title":"NOAA National Ocean Service — Latitude and Longitude","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/latitude.html","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Latitude describes north–south position using the Equator as reference. Longitude describes east–west position using a reference meridian.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-080","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-080","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Why does local solar time change as longitude changes?","value":"Earth rotates, bringing different meridians towards the Sun in turn","group":"Longitude and local time","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Royal Museums Greenwich — Longitude","verificationUrl":"https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/topics/what-longitude","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G08","title":"Royal Museums Greenwich — Longitude","url":"https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/topics/what-longitude","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"East–west position is connected to the timing of the Sun’s apparent movement. Civil time zones are human arrangements and do not follow meridians perfectly.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-081","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-081","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which seasonal pattern is characteristic of a Mediterranean climate?","value":"Generally dry summers and wetter, mild winters","group":"Mediterranean climate","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UK Met Office — Climate zones","verificationUrl":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/weather/climate/climate-explained/climate-zones","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G09","title":"UK Met Office — Climate zones","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/weather/climate/climate-explained/climate-zones","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The term refers to a climatic pattern found beyond the Mediterranean region as well. It does not mean that every day in winter is wet or every summer day is rainless.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-082","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-082","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"What does urbanization usually mean in population studies?","value":"An increasing proportion of people living in urban areas","group":"Urbanization","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"United Nations Population Division — World Urbanization Prospects","verificationUrl":"https://population.un.org/wup/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"G10","title":"United Nations Population Division — World Urbanization Prospects","url":"https://population.un.org/wup/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Urbanization concerns the urban share of a population. It is not identical to the physical expansion of a single city or to overall population growth.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-083","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-083","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"What is the central journey in Homer’s Odyssey?","value":"Odysseus’s return home after the Trojan War","group":"Odyssey","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University — Homer, Odyssey","verificationUrl":"https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0136","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L01","title":"Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University — Homer, Odyssey","url":"https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0136","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Odyssey follows a difficult homecoming, while the Iliad centres on an episode of the Trojan War itself. The two epics are related but not interchangeable.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-085","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-085","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Don Quixote’s adventures repeatedly expose the gap between what?","value":"Romantic ideals of chivalry and ordinary reality","group":"Don Quixote","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Biblioteca Virtual Miguel de Cervantes — Don Quijote","verificationUrl":"https://www.cervantesvirtual.com/portales/miguel_de_cervantes/su_obra_quijote/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L03","title":"Biblioteca Virtual Miguel de Cervantes — Don Quijote","url":"https://www.cervantesvirtual.com/portales/miguel_de_cervantes/su_obra_quijote/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Cervantes’s hero interprets the world through books of chivalry, often misrecognizing what he encounters. This tension drives both the humour and the novel’s larger questions.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-086","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-086","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"In Mary Shelley’s novel, who is Victor Frankenstein?","value":"The person who creates the living creature","group":"Frankenstein","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Bodleian Libraries — The Shelley-Godwin Archive","verificationUrl":"http://shelleygodwinarchive.org/contents/frankenstein/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L04","title":"Bodleian Libraries — The Shelley-Godwin Archive","url":"http://shelleygodwinarchive.org/contents/frankenstein/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Frankenstein is the creator’s surname. Confusing creator and creature can obscure the novel’s questions about responsibility for what one brings into the world.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-087","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-087","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which concern is central to George Orwell’s Nineteen Eighty-Four?","value":"Political control through surveillance and manipulation of truth","group":"Nineteen Eighty-Four","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Orwell Foundation — Nineteen Eighty-Four","verificationUrl":"https://www.orwellfoundation.com/the-orwell-foundation/orwell/books-by-orwell/nineteen-eighty-four/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L05","title":"The Orwell Foundation — Nineteen Eighty-Four","url":"https://www.orwellfoundation.com/the-orwell-foundation/orwell/books-by-orwell/nineteen-eighty-four/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Orwell’s dystopia links political domination to control over information, language and memory. It is not a neutral prediction of every feature of the actual year 1984.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-088","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-088","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which issue is central to Ibsen’s A Doll’s House?","value":"Individual autonomy and the expectations of marriage","group":"A Doll’s House","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"University of Oslo — Henrik Ibsen’s writings: A Doll’s House","verificationUrl":"https://www.ibsen.uio.no/DRVIT_Du%7CDuht.xhtml","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L06","title":"University of Oslo — Henrik Ibsen’s writings: A Doll’s House","url":"https://www.ibsen.uio.no/DRVIT_Du%7CDuht.xhtml","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The play examines the roles assigned within marriage and the conflict between social expectations and personal independence. Its title is not an indication that it is a children’s puppet play.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-089","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-089","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which of these works is an opera?","value":"Mozart’s The Magic Flute","group":"Opera recognition","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"English National Opera — The Magic Flute","verificationUrl":"https://www.eno.org/operas/the-magic-flute/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L07","title":"English National Opera — The Magic Flute","url":"https://www.eno.org/operas/the-magic-flute/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Magic Flute is a staged dramatic work with singing. The other choices are instrumental works, even though their forms and instrumental forces differ.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-090","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-090","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"What is a typical feature of a solo concerto?","value":"A featured solo instrument interacts with an orchestra","group":"Concerto","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Philharmonia Orchestra — Musical Dictionary","verificationUrl":"https://philharmonia.co.uk/resources/musical-dictionary/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L08","title":"Philharmonia Orchestra — Musical Dictionary","url":"https://philharmonia.co.uk/resources/musical-dictionary/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The relationship between soloist and ensemble is central to the solo concerto. It is a musical form, not merely a synonym for any public concert.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-091","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-091","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which tendency is associated with Italian neorealist cinema?","value":"Stories of ordinary people filmed with an emphasis on everyday social conditions","group":"Italian neorealism","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"British Film Institute — A beginner’s guide to Italian neorealism","verificationUrl":"https://www.bfi.org.uk/features/beginners-guide-italian-neorealism","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L09","title":"British Film Institute — A beginner’s guide to Italian neorealism","url":"https://www.bfi.org.uk/features/beginners-guide-italian-neorealism","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Neorealist films often engage postwar social life, locations and ordinary people. These are tendencies rather than an absolute rule about every actor or every shot.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-092","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-092","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"What were intertitles used for in many silent films?","value":"Displaying dialogue or information on text cards between shots","group":"Silent-film intertitles","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Library of Congress — Silent Film","verificationUrl":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/national-screening-room/about-this-collection/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"L10","title":"Library of Congress — Silent Film","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/national-screening-room/about-this-collection/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Silent films could convey written dialogue and narrative information through intertitles. “Silent” therefore does not mean that audiences saw no language on screen.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-094","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-094","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Marie Curie’s research is especially associated with which phenomenon?","value":"Radioactivity","group":"Marie Curie","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Nobel Prize — Marie Curie: Facts","verificationUrl":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1903/marie-curie/facts/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"T02","title":"Nobel Prize — Marie Curie: Facts","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1903/marie-curie/facts/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Curie’s work on radioactivity included investigations of radioactive elements. Recognizing her field matters more here than memorizing a list of biographical dates.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-096","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-096","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"What did the World Wide Web add to the use of computer networks?","value":"A system for linking and accessing documents through hypertext","group":"World Wide Web","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"CERN — The birth of the Web","verificationUrl":"https://home.cern/science/computing/birth-web","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"T04","title":"CERN — The birth of the Web","url":"https://home.cern/science/computing/birth-web","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Web is a service built on network infrastructure, not another name for the entire Internet. Its linked documents made information sharing much easier.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-097","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-097","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Greenhouse gases warm Earth’s surface and lower atmosphere mainly by interacting with what?","value":"Outgoing infrared radiation","group":"Greenhouse effect","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"NASA — What is the greenhouse effect?","verificationUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/climate-change/faq/what-is-the-greenhouse-effect/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"T05","title":"NASA — What is the greenhouse effect?","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/climate-change/faq/what-is-the-greenhouse-effect/","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The surface emits infrared radiation after absorbing energy. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit infrared radiation, affecting how energy leaves the climate system.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-101","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-101","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"How were many monumental tomb facades at Petra made?","value":"By carving the natural rock face","group":"Petra rock-cut architecture","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Petra","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A01","title":"UNESCO — Petra","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/","support_summary":"Petra includes monumental tomb and temple facades carved directly into the rock.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Rock-cut architecture removes material from a cliff instead of assembling every wall from separate blocks. Petra also contains freestanding buildings, so the claim is not about every structure at the site.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-102","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-102","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Borobudur in Java is principally associated with which religious tradition?","value":"Buddhism","group":"Borobudur religious tradition","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Borobudur Temple Compounds","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A02","title":"UNESCO — Borobudur Temple Compounds","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/","support_summary":"Borobudur is a Buddhist monument in Java; its terraces and stupas express Buddhist cosmology.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Its stupas and Buddha images form a Buddhist sacred monument. The arrangement of its terraces gives architectural form to religious ideas, rather than merely providing a viewing platform.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-103","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-103","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Angkor Wat belongs to the architectural heritage of which empire?","value":"The Khmer Empire","group":"Angkor Khmer heritage","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Angkor","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A03","title":"UNESCO — Angkor","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/","support_summary":"Angkor contains capitals and temples of the Khmer Empire, including Angkor Wat.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Angkor was a centre of Khmer power in Southeast Asia. Associating the monument with its civilization is more useful than memorizing the dates of individual restorations.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-104","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-104","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What was the central building of the Taj Mahal complex created to be?","value":"A mausoleum","group":"Taj Mahal funerary function","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Taj Mahal","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A04","title":"UNESCO — Taj Mahal","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252/","support_summary":"Shah Jahan built the white marble mausoleum in memory of Mumtaz Mahal.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Shah Jahan commissioned the monument in memory of Mumtaz Mahal. Although the complex contains other buildings, the famous central marble building is a tomb.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-106","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-106","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which civilization built Machu Picchu?","value":"The Inca","group":"Machu Picchu civilization","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Historic Sanctuary of Machu Picchu","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A06","title":"UNESCO — Historic Sanctuary of Machu Picchu","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/","support_summary":"Machu Picchu represents Inca urban and architectural achievement in a mountain setting.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Machu Picchu belongs to the Inca world of the Andes. The Maya and Aztec developed in Mesoamerica, not in the Andean region.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-107","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-107","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Many churches at Lalibela were made by:","value":"Cutting into living rock","group":"Lalibela monolithic construction","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A07","title":"UNESCO — Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/","support_summary":"The churches were hewn from living rock; columns, doors and roofs were carved from monolithic blocks.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Their forms were excavated from the rock rather than assembled as ordinary masonry walls.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-108","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-108","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which combination describes Himeji Castle most accurately?","value":"Timber construction with plastered walls","group":"Himeji structural tradition","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Himeji-jo","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A08","title":"UNESCO — Himeji-jo","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/","support_summary":"Himeji is Japanese defensive castle architecture in wood, with white plastered earthen walls.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Himeji is a major example of Japanese castle construction in wood. Its familiar white appearance comes from plastered surfaces, not from an entirely marble structure.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-111","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-111","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which material was central to Auguste Perret’s post-war rebuilding of Le Havre?","value":"Reinforced concrete","group":"Le Havre postwar reconstruction","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Le Havre, the City Rebuilt by Auguste Perret","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1181/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A11","title":"UNESCO — Le Havre, the City Rebuilt by Auguste Perret","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1181/","support_summary":"The post-war rebuilding used a modular grid and reinforced concrete systematically.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Perret used reinforced concrete within a coordinated modular urban scheme. The reconstruction is significant as a city-scale design, not just as a collection of isolated monuments.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-112","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-112","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which setting most directly explains the historic street-and-canal pattern of Venice?","value":"A group of islands within a lagoon","group":"Venice lagoon urban form","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Venice and its Lagoon","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/394/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A12","title":"UNESCO — Venice and its Lagoon","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/394/","support_summary":"Venice developed on small islands in a lagoon, with canals as the arteries of the urban system.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Venice grew on small islands whose water channels became part of its circulation system. Its urban form reflects adaptation to the lagoon.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-114","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-114","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which project is an example of adaptive reuse?","value":"Converting an existing mill into a library while retaining its significant fabric","group":"Adaptive reuse versus reconstruction","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Park Service — Standards for Rehabilitation","verificationUrl":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/taxincentives/secretarys-standards-rehabilitation.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A14","title":"National Park Service — Standards for Rehabilitation","url":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/taxincentives/secretarys-standards-rehabilitation.htm","support_summary":"A compatible new use may retain significant historic features; additions should not falsify historical development.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Adaptive reuse gives an existing building a compatible new function. Retaining significant fabric distinguishes it from demolition followed by replacement.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-120","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-120","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pair correctly relates Roman entertainment buildings to their typical arrangement?","value":"Theatre — seating facing a stage; amphitheatre — seating around an arena","group":"Roman theatre and amphitheatre","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Roman theatre (structure)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_theatre_(structure)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Roman_theatre","key":"Roman_theatre","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Roman theatre (structure)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_theatre_(structure)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Colosseum","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Colosseum","key":"Colosseum","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Colosseum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Theatres generally placed spectators opposite a stage. Amphitheatres enclosed an arena with seating around it, while circuses were associated with chariot racing.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-121","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-121","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What role does a cloister usually have in a monastery?","value":"Covered circulation around an open courtyard","group":"Cloister and monastic circulation","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Cloister","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloister","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Cloister","key":"Cloister","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Cloister","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloister","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"A cloister links covered walks around a court with adjacent monastic buildings. It is both a spatial arrangement and a circulation system, not the courtyard alone.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-122","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-122","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"A minaret is traditionally associated with which activity?","value":"Calling the faithful to prayer","group":"Minaret function","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Merriam-Webster — minaret","verificationUrl":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/minaret","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-A22","title":"Merriam-Webster — minaret","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/minaret","support_summary":"A minaret is a tower associated with a mosque and traditionally used for the call to prayer.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"editorial_dictionary","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"A minaret is a tower associated with a mosque. It differs from the mihrab, which is the prayer-direction niche inside the mosque.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-123","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-123","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The perspectival vanishing point in Leonardo’s Last Supper is located near:","value":"Christ’s head","group":"Leonardo and perspectival focus","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Last Supper (Leonardo)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Supper_(Leonardo)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Last_Supper","key":"Last_Supper","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Last Supper (Leonardo)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Supper_(Leonardo)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The orthogonals converge near Christ, reinforcing his central position.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-124","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-124","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The receding landscape in these portraits illustrates:","value":"Atmospheric perspective","group":"Piero and atmospheric depth","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Diptych of Federico da Montefeltro and Battista Sforza","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diptych_of_Federico_da_Montefeltro_and_Battista_Sforza","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Urbino_diptych","key":"Urbino_diptych","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Diptych of Federico da Montefeltro and Battista Sforza","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diptych_of_Federico_da_Montefeltro_and_Battista_Sforza","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Aerial perspective","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerial_perspective","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Atmospheric_perspective","key":"Atmospheric_perspective","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Aerial perspective","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerial_perspective","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Distant forms become cooler, paler and less sharply contrasted.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-129","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-129","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Piranesi’s Carceri are a series of:","value":"Etchings","group":"Piranesi Carceri: print medium and historical imagination","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Met — Title Page, Carceri d’invenzione","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/362797","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Piranesi","key":"Piranesi","title":"The Met — Title Page, Carceri d’invenzione","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/362797","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"The Met identifies this title plate, c.1749–1750, as etching, engraving and sulphur tint/open bite; the object image is labelled Public Domain.","retrieval_reference":"turn259132view0","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn993414view1","verification_record_origin":"resumed_web_read"}}],"explanation74":"These eighteenth-century prints imagine monumental, labyrinthine prison spaces.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-131","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-131","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which pair of artists is closely associated with early Fauvism?","value":"Henri Matisse and André Derain","group":"Fauvism principal artists","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Fauvism","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/fauvism","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-R09","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Fauvism","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/fauvism","support_summary":"Henri Matisse and André Derain worked together in 1905 and were central to the development of Fauvism.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Matisse and Derain developed an approach using vivid, non-naturalistic colour. Recognizing the pair helps locate Fauvism among the different movements of modern art.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-135","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-135","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which contrast between mosaic and buon fresco is correct?","value":"Mosaic — assembled tesserae; buon fresco — pigment applied to fresh plaster","group":"Mosaic and fresco comparison","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Mosaic","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosaic","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Mosaic","key":"Mosaic","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Mosaic","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosaic","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fresco","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fresco","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Fresco","key":"Fresco","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fresco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fresco","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Mosaic constructs an image from small pieces such as stone or glass. Buon fresco applies pigments to fresh plaster. They can decorate similar architectural spaces while using fundamentally different processes.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-138","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-138","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which subject would most naturally be classified as a still life?","value":"A table arranged with a jug, fruit and folded cloth","group":"Still life and portrait distinction","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Gallery — Still life","verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/still-lives","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-R15","title":"National Gallery — Still life","url":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/glossary/still-lives","support_summary":"Still-life painting represents arranged inanimate objects.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Still life concentrates on inanimate objects arranged as a pictorial subject. A painting may include a table within another genre, so the main subject matters.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-142","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-142","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What did the American Declaration of Independence of 1776 proclaim?","value":"Separation of the colonies from the British Crown","group":"American Declaration political purpose","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"US National Archives — Declaration of Independence transcript","verificationUrl":"https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/declaration-transcript","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-H04","title":"US National Archives — Declaration of Independence transcript","url":"https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/declaration-transcript","support_summary":"The declaration states that the colonies are free and independent states, absolved from allegiance to the British Crown.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The document declared the colonies independent states. Its language of rights did not mean that all inhabitants immediately enjoyed equal rights in practice.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-143","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-143","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"At which battle was Napoleon defeated at the end of the Hundred Days?","value":"Waterloo","group":"Waterloo and Napoleon","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Army Museum — Battle of Waterloo","verificationUrl":"https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/battle-waterloo","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-H05","title":"National Army Museum — Battle of Waterloo","url":"https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/battle-waterloo","support_summary":"The defeat at Waterloo in 1815 ended Napoleon’s return to power.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Waterloo was fought in 1815 after Napoleon returned from exile. It belongs to the end of the Napoleonic era, not to the wars of the twentieth century.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-145","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-145","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"What changed with Britain’s Equal Franchise Act of 1928?","value":"Women gained parliamentary voting rights on the same terms as men","group":"British women equal franchise 1928","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UK Parliament — Women and the vote","verificationUrl":"https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/transformingsociety/electionsvoting/womenvote/overview/thevote/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-H07","title":"UK Parliament — Women and the vote","url":"https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/transformingsociety/electionsvoting/womenvote/overview/thevote/","support_summary":"Women first gained a restricted parliamentary franchise in 1918; the 1928 Act gave women voting rights on the same terms as men.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Some women had obtained the parliamentary vote in 1918 under unequal conditions. The 1928 reform removed the sex-based difference in the franchise terms.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-146","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-146","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which two independent states emerged from the partition of British India in 1947?","value":"India and Pakistan","group":"Partition of British India","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Army Museum — Independence and Partition, 1947","verificationUrl":"https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/independence-and-partition-1947","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-H08","title":"National Army Museum — Independence and Partition, 1947","url":"https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/independence-and-partition-1947","support_summary":"The end of British rule in 1947 brought the independent states of India and Pakistan, accompanied by mass displacement and violence.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Pakistan initially included western and eastern parts; the eastern part later became Bangladesh. Independence and partition also brought enormous human suffering through displacement and violence.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-149","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-149","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which description identifies the Holocaust most precisely?","value":"The Nazi-led genocide of European Jews","group":"Holocaust historical definition","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"United States Holocaust Memorial Museum — Introduction to the Holocaust","verificationUrl":"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/introduction-to-the-holocaust","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-H11","title":"United States Holocaust Memorial Museum — Introduction to the Holocaust","url":"https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/introduction-to-the-holocaust","support_summary":"The Holocaust was the systematic persecution and murder of six million European Jews by Nazi Germany and its allies and collaborators.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Nazi Germany, its allies and collaborators systematically persecuted and murdered European Jews. Other groups were also victims of Nazi persecution and mass murder; the term has a specific historical meaning.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-157","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-157","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which US constitutional amendment abolished slavery, subject to its stated punishment-for-crime exception?","value":"The Thirteenth Amendment","group":"Thirteenth Amendment and legal slavery","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"US National Archives — 13th Amendment","verificationUrl":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/13th-amendment","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-H19","title":"US National Archives — 13th Amendment","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/13th-amendment","support_summary":"The amendment abolished slavery and involuntary servitude in the United States, with the stated punishment-for-crime exception.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Thirteenth Amendment made abolition part of the Constitution. The First concerns freedoms such as speech, while the Nineteenth concerns voting rights regardless of sex.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-159","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-159","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which institution mainly provides development finance and expertise to reduce poverty?","value":"The World Bank","group":"World Bank development role","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"World Bank Group — Who We Are","verificationUrl":"https://www.worldbank.org/ext/en/who-we-are","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E01","title":"World Bank Group — Who We Are","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/ext/en/who-we-are","support_summary":"The World Bank Group combines financing, knowledge and expertise to reduce poverty and support development.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Development finance can support infrastructure and public services. The World Bank is not a world government or a universal criminal court.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-160","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-160","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which UN organization has a specific mandate focused on children?","value":"UNICEF","group":"UNICEF children mandate","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNICEF — What We Do","verificationUrl":"https://www.unicef.org/what-we-do","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E02","title":"UNICEF — What We Do","url":"https://www.unicef.org/what-we-do","support_summary":"UNICEF protects children’s rights and supports health, nutrition, education and protection.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"UNICEF works on children’s rights, survival, education and protection. Its remit is broader than emergency food deliveries alone.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-161","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-161","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which organization principally coordinates international public-health work?","value":"WHO","group":"WHO international health role","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"WHO — About WHO","verificationUrl":"https://www.who.int/about","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E03","title":"WHO — About WHO","url":"https://www.who.int/about","support_summary":"WHO directs and coordinates international public-health work.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The World Health Organization is concerned with health. The alternatives focus respectively on trade, collective security, petroleum cooperation and the Olympic movement.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-162","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-162","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which three groups are represented in the ILO’s tripartite structure?","value":"Governments, employers and workers","group":"ILO tripartite representation","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"ILO — About the ILO","verificationUrl":"https://www.ilo.org/about-ilo","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E04","title":"ILO — About the ILO","url":"https://www.ilo.org/about-ilo","support_summary":"The ILO brings together governments, employers and workers in its tripartite structure.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Including workers and employers alongside governments makes labour standards a subject of organized social dialogue. “Tripartite” refers to these three participant groups.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-163","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-163","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which action by a permanent Security Council member can block a substantive draft resolution?","value":"Casting a negative vote","group":"UN Security Council veto and abstention","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UN Security Council — Voting System","verificationUrl":"https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/content/voting-system","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E05","title":"UN Security Council — Voting System","url":"https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/content/voting-system","support_summary":"A negative vote by a permanent member can block a substantive draft resolution; abstention is not itself a veto.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The veto concerns a permanent member’s negative vote on a non-procedural matter. An abstention is not the same thing as exercising a veto.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-165","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-165","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"How are members of the European Parliament chosen?","value":"By direct elections among EU voters","group":"European Parliament electoral legitimacy","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"European Union — European Parliament","verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/search-all-eu-institutions-and-bodies/european-parliament_en","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E07","title":"European Union — European Parliament","url":"https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/search-all-eu-institutions-and-bodies/european-parliament_en","support_summary":"The European Parliament is directly elected by EU voters.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Parliament has a directly elected membership. It is not the same body as a meeting of national ministers or heads of government.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-167","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-167","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which measure is an example of fiscal rather than monetary policy?","value":"A government increasing spending on public schools","group":"Fiscal versus monetary policy","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"IMF — Fiscal Policy: Taking and Giving Away","verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/Fiscal-Policy","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E09","title":"IMF — Fiscal Policy: Taking and Giving Away","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/Fiscal-Policy","support_summary":"Fiscal policy uses government spending and taxation; central-bank policy is monetary policy.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Fiscal policy concerns government taxation and spending. Monetary policy concerns money and financial conditions and is normally conducted by a central bank.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-169","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-169","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which statement correctly distinguishes an annual budget deficit from public debt?","value":"The deficit is a shortfall over a year; debt is an outstanding amount owed","group":"Budget deficit versus debt stock","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Merriam-Webster — Deficit","verificationUrl":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/deficit","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E11","title":"Merriam-Webster — Deficit","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/deficit","support_summary":"A budget deficit is expenditure exceeding revenue.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"editorial_dictionary","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}},{"file":"Merriam-Webster — National Debt","verificationUrl":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/national%20debt","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E12","title":"Merriam-Webster — National Debt","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/national%20debt","support_summary":"National debt is the total money owed by a national government.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"editorial_dictionary","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"One concept concerns flows during a period and the other a stock at a point in time. Balancing this year’s budget does not automatically repay earlier borrowing.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-173","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-173","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which pairing of mountain range and geographical setting is correct?","value":"Pyrenees — France and Spain; Apennines — the Italian peninsula","group":"European mountain systems and political geography","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Pyrénées–Mont Perdu","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/773/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Pyrenees","key":"Pyrenees","title":"UNESCO — Pyrénées–Mont Perdu","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/773/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"UNESCO describes the Pyrenees/Mont Perdu landscape as spanning the France–Spain border.","retrieval_reference":"turn259132view2","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"resumed_web_read","retrieval_record":"turn315344view0"}},{"file":"UNESCO — Appennino Tosco-Emiliano Biosphere Reserve","verificationUrl":"https://www.unesco.org/en/mab/appennino-tosco-emiliano","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Apennines","key":"Apennines","title":"UNESCO — Appennino Tosco-Emiliano Biosphere Reserve","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/mab/appennino-tosco-emiliano","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"UNESCO states that the Apennine range forms the backbone of the Italian peninsula.","retrieval_reference":"turn976976view2","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"resumed_web_read","retrieval_record":"turn315344view1"}}],"explanation74":"The Pyrenees form a mountain barrier between France and Spain, while the Apennines run along the Italian peninsula. The pairing connects physical regions with political geography; the Alps lie farther north, across several European countries.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-174","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-174","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which process can turn a river meander into an oxbow lake?","value":"The river cuts across the meander neck and isolates the former loop","group":"Oxbow lakes and river change","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Oxbow lake","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxbow_lake","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Oxbow","key":"Oxbow","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Oxbow lake","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxbow_lake","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"When a river cuts through a meander neck, deposition can separate the abandoned loop from the active channel. The resulting lake records a former river course rather than volcanic or glacial excavation.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-176","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-176","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which mountain system is shared by Peru, Bolivia and Chile?","value":"The Andes","group":"Andes regional association","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Qhapaq Ñan, Andean Road System","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1459/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-G04","title":"UNESCO — Qhapaq Ñan, Andean Road System","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1459/","support_summary":"The Andean road system extends through countries including Peru, Bolivia and Chile along the Andes.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Andes form a major mountain system along western South America. They also provided the setting for extensive networks of movement and settlement.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-181","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-181","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"What is the immediate natural source of geothermal energy?","value":"Heat inside Earth","group":"Geothermal resource origin","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"US Department of Energy — Geothermal Basics","verificationUrl":"https://www.energy.gov/hgeo/geothermal/geothermal-basics","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-G09","title":"US Department of Energy — Geothermal Basics","url":"https://www.energy.gov/hgeo/geothermal/geothermal-basics","support_summary":"Geothermal energy uses heat within Earth.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Geothermal energy draws on Earth’s internal heat. It is not the same process as generating power from sunlight, wind or the combustion of fossil fuels.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-182","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-182","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which description defines an isthmus rather than a strait?","value":"A narrow strip of land joining two larger land areas","group":"Isthmus versus strait","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Merriam-Webster — Isthmus","verificationUrl":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/isthmus","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-G10","title":"Merriam-Webster — Isthmus","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/isthmus","support_summary":"An isthmus is a narrow land strip connecting larger land areas, with water on each side.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"editorial_dictionary","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"An isthmus is land, whereas a strait is water between land masses. Keeping the contrast clear helps explain the geographical setting of canals and shipping routes.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-195","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-195","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which function is a transistor designed to perform in an electronic circuit?","value":"Amplifying or switching electrical signals","group":"Transistor role in electronics","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Computer History Museum — Invention of the Point-Contact Transistor","verificationUrl":"https://www.computerhistory.org/siliconengine/invention-of-the-point-contact-transistor/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S03","title":"Computer History Museum — Invention of the Point-Contact Transistor","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/siliconengine/invention-of-the-point-contact-transistor/","support_summary":"Transistors developed as semiconductor devices for amplification and switching, replacing functions of vacuum tubes and electromechanical switches.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Transistors control electrical signals and underlie modern electronic circuits. Their significance is not that they create energy, but that they can control a current using another signal.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-196","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-196","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which of these is NOT a fossil fuel?","value":"Uranium","group":"Fossil versus nuclear fuel","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"US EIA — Sources of energy","verificationUrl":"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S04","title":"US EIA — Sources of energy","url":"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php","support_summary":"Petroleum, natural gas and coal are fossil fuels; uranium is a nonrenewable nuclear fuel, not a fossil fuel.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Uranium supplies nuclear energy, whereas the other choices are fossil fuels or, in the case of lignite, a type of coal. Nonrenewable does not mean the same thing as fossil.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-197","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-197","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which gas makes up the largest share of Earth’s atmosphere near the surface?","value":"Nitrogen","group":"Main gas in Earth atmosphere","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"NASA — Earth Facts: Atmosphere","verificationUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S05","title":"NASA — Earth Facts: Atmosphere","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/","support_summary":"Near the surface, Earth’s atmosphere consists of about 78% nitrogen and 21% oxygen.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Nitrogen makes up about 78% of dry air, while oxygen accounts for about 21%. A gas can have important biological or climatic effects without being the most abundant.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-198","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-198","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Why do observers on Earth see approximately the same face of the Moon?","value":"Its rotation period matches its orbital period around Earth","group":"Moon synchronous rotation","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"NASA — Moon Facts: Orbit and Rotation","verificationUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S06","title":"NASA — Moon Facts: Orbit and Rotation","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/","support_summary":"The Moon rotates once in the same time that it completes one orbit around Earth, explaining why roughly the same face is visible.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"The Moon does rotate. Turning once during each orbit keeps roughly the same hemisphere facing Earth; the far side is not permanently dark.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-199","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-199","anchor":"MAJOR:DESIGN","subject":"Which design movement is strongly associated with interwar stylized geometry and zigzag ornament?","value":"Art Deco","group":"Art Deco interwar visual vocabulary","major":"Design","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Victoria and Albert Museum — An introduction to Art Deco","verificationUrl":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/an-introduction-to-art-deco","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-D01","title":"Victoria and Albert Museum — An introduction to Art Deco","url":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/an-introduction-to-art-deco","support_summary":"Art Deco developed decorative forms involving stylized geometry and zigzags; the 1925 Paris exhibition was a major showcase.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"explanation74":"Art Deco drew on modern visual forms while also making decorative and luxury objects. Its geometric vocabulary is a useful clue, not a rule that every work must display a zigzag.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-201","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-201","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pair correctly identifies the ornament of the two classical orders?","value":"Ionic — volutes; Corinthian — acanthus leaves","group":"Classical capital comparison","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Ancient Greek architecture","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Greek_architecture","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Greek_architecture","key":"Greek_architecture","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Ancient Greek architecture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Greek_architecture","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Volutes are the scrolls of an Ionic capital; stylized acanthus leaves characterize a Corinthian capital. Triglyphs belong to the Doric frieze, not a capital.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-202","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-202","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pairing distinguishes two similarly named ancient monuments?","value":"Parthenon — classical Athens; Pantheon — imperial Rome","group":"Parthenon and Pantheon civilizations","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Acropolis, Athens","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/404/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Acropolis","key":"Acropolis","title":"UNESCO — Acropolis, Athens","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/404/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Pericles rebuilding after the Persian Wars; fifth-century monuments including the Parthenon.","retrieval_reference":"turn924909view0","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pantheon, Rome","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantheon,_Rome","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Pantheon","key":"Pantheon","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pantheon, Rome","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantheon,_Rome","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The Parthenon is a fifth-century-BCE Athenian temple. The standing Pantheon belongs chiefly to Hadrian's second-century-CE rebuilding in Rome.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-205","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-205","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which Roman building is correctly matched with its main ancient use?","value":"Circus Maximus — chariot racing","group":"Roman spectacle buildings","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Circus Maximus","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circus_Maximus","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Circus_Maximus","key":"Circus_Maximus","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Circus Maximus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circus_Maximus","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Colosseum","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Colosseum","key":"Colosseum","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Colosseum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The circus provided a long racing course; an amphitheatre enclosed an arena. Roman baths and basilicas served different social and civic functions.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-209","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-209","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Justinian and Theodora appear in Ravenna's San Vitale mosaics because the city was linked to which power?","value":"The Byzantine Empire","group":"San Vitale and imperial representation","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Early Christian Monuments of Ravenna","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/788/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Ravenna","key":"Ravenna","title":"UNESCO — Early Christian Monuments of Ravenna","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/788/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Ravenna was a Roman and later Byzantine centre, with important fifth- and sixth-century Christian monuments and mosaics.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn896906view1","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Basilica of San Vitale","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_San_Vitale","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-San_Vitale","key":"San_Vitale","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Basilica of San Vitale","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_San_Vitale","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The sixth-century mosaics place imperial figures within a Christian sacred setting. Ravenna was an important centre of Byzantine authority in Italy.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-210","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-210","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which patron and period are associated with Hagia Sophia's principal Byzantine construction?","value":"Justinian I — sixth century CE","group":"Hagia Sophia and Justinian","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Hagia Sophia","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hagia_Sophia","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Hagia_Sophia","key":"Hagia_Sophia","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Hagia Sophia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hagia_Sophia","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Justinian commissioned the great church completed in 537. Later Ottoman conversion and additions are separate phases in the building's history.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-211","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-211","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Sainte-Chapelle in Paris was commissioned to house:","value":"Relics of the Passion","group":"Sainte Chapelle royal context","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Centre des monuments nationaux — History of the Sainte-Chapelle","verificationUrl":"https://www.sainte-chapelle.fr/en/discover/history-of-the-sainte-chapelle","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Sainte_Chapelle","key":"Sainte_Chapelle","title":"Centre des monuments nationaux — History of the Sainte-Chapelle","url":"https://www.sainte-chapelle.fr/en/discover/history-of-the-sainte-chapelle","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_search_excerpt_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Louis IX and the royal chapel built to house Passion relics.","retrieval_reference":"turn355094search22","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"Louis IX commissioned the chapel to house relics including the Crown of Thorns.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-213","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-213","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Wren’s rebuilding of Saint Paul’s Cathedral followed the:","value":"Great Fire of London","group":"St Pauls rebuilding historical context","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"St Paul’s Cathedral — Our timeline","verificationUrl":"https://www.stpauls.co.uk/our-timeline","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-StPauls","key":"StPauls","title":"St Paul’s Cathedral — Our timeline","url":"https://www.stpauls.co.uk/our-timeline","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_search_excerpt_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"The Great Fire of 1666 and Christopher Wren’s rebuilding.","retrieval_reference":"turn247313search16","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"The 1666 fire destroyed the previous cathedral; Wren designed its replacement.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-214","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-214","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The Pazzi Chapel belongs to which architectural period?","value":"Early Renaissance","group":"Pazzi Chapel spatial vocabulary","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pazzi Chapel","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pazzi_Chapel","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Pazzi","key":"Pazzi","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pazzi Chapel","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pazzi_Chapel","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The Florentine chapel uses ordered geometric proportions and classical elements.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-215","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-215","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which family and architect are associated with the original palace shown?","value":"Medici — Michelozzo","group":"Medici palace patronage","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Palazzo Medici Riccardi — Il Palazzo","verificationUrl":"https://www.palazzomediciriccardi.it/il-palazzo/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Medici_palace","key":"Medici_palace","title":"Palazzo Medici Riccardi — Il Palazzo","url":"https://www.palazzomediciriccardi.it/il-palazzo/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_search_excerpt_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Cosimo de’ Medici, Michelozzo and the 1444 commission.","retrieval_reference":"turn355094search4","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"Palazzo Medici Riccardi was commissioned by Cosimo de' Medici from Michelozzo. It is a Florentine Renaissance family palace, not a Roman papal villa.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-217","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-217","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pair correctly matches a writer with an architectural treatise?","value":"Alberti — De re aedificatoria; Palladio — The Four Books of Architecture","group":"Renaissance architectural treatises","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — De re aedificatoria","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_re_aedificatoria","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Alberti","key":"Alberti","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — De re aedificatoria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_re_aedificatoria","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — I quattro libri dell'architettura","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_quattro_libri_dell%27architettura","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Palladio_books","key":"Palladio_books","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — I quattro libri dell'architettura","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_quattro_libri_dell%27architettura","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Alberti's Latin treatise revived and reworked classical architectural theory. Palladio's illustrated Four Books appeared in 1570; Vitruvius wrote De architectura in antiquity.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-218","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-218","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Schinkel’s Altes Museum was conceived as a:","value":"Public art museum","group":"Altes Museum: public institution and classical language","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Staatliche Museen zu Berlin — Altes Museum","verificationUrl":"https://www.smb.museum/en/museums-institutions/altes-museum/home/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Altes","key":"Altes","title":"Staatliche Museen zu Berlin — Altes Museum","url":"https://www.smb.museum/en/museums-institutions/altes-museum/home/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_search_excerpt_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Karl Friedrich Schinkel and the neoclassical museum building.","retrieval_reference":"turn378440search10","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"The Berlin museum gives a civic cultural institution a monumental classical front.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-219","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-219","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"In the Vitruvian triad, utilitas adds which concern to strength and beauty?","value":"Fitness for use","group":"Vitruvian triad","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Pritzker Architecture Prize — Vitruvian principles","verificationUrl":"https://www.pritzkerprize.com/about","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Vitruvius","key":"Vitruvius","title":"Pritzker Architecture Prize — Vitruvian principles","url":"https://www.pritzkerprize.com/about","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"The medallion section explicitly associates firmitas, utilitas, venustas with firmness, commodity and delight.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn913989view2","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"Firmitas, utilitas and venustas concern stability, usefulness and beauty. A structurally sound and attractive building must also serve the activities for which it is made.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-221","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-221","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pairing distinguishes the main designer from the designer of the Teatro Olimpico's permanent perspective scenery?","value":"Andrea Palladio — theatre; Vincenzo Scamozzi — scenery","group":"Teatro Olimpico division of authorship","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Teatro Olimpico","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teatro_Olimpico","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Teatro_Olimpico","key":"Teatro_Olimpico","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Teatro Olimpico","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teatro_Olimpico","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Palladio designed the theatre in Vicenza. After his death, Scamozzi created the famous streets in forced perspective behind the stage facade.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-222","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-222","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Bramante's Tempietto adapts which ancient architectural idea to a Christian memorial?","value":"A circular temple surrounded by columns","group":"Tempietto classical form","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — San Pietro in Montorio","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pietro_in_Montorio","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Tempietto","key":"Tempietto","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — San Pietro in Montorio","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pietro_in_Montorio","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The Tempietto at San Pietro in Montorio uses a centralized, column-ringed form. The classical reference serves a Christian commemorative purpose.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-226","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-226","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The concave and convex façade shown is characteristic of:","value":"Roman Baroque","group":"Borromini facade and spatial movement","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Carlo_alle_Quattro_Fontane","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-San_Carlo","key":"San_Carlo","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Carlo_alle_Quattro_Fontane","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Borromini’s San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane gives the façade a continuous undulating rhythm.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-227","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-227","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which historical and institutional setting belongs with the church shown?","value":"A university church in seventeenth-century Rome","group":"Sant’Ivo: university church and Roman Baroque","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sant%27Ivo_alla_Sapienza","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Sant_Ivo","key":"Sant_Ivo","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sant%27Ivo_alla_Sapienza","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza was designed by Borromini for the Sapienza university complex in seventeenth-century Rome. The spiral lantern belongs to this Roman Baroque setting, not to a freestanding medieval bell tower.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-228","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-228","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Juvarra’s Basilica of Superga is associated with which dynasty?","value":"House of Savoy","group":"Superga context","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Basilica of Superga","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_Superga","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Superga","key":"Superga","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Basilica of Superga","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_Superga","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Superga overlooks Turin and was commissioned under Victor Amadeus II of Savoy.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-230","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-230","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pairing links the church shown with its architect and city?","value":"Palladio — Venice","group":"Il Redentore and civic ritual","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Il Redentore","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Il_Redentore","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Redentore","key":"Redentore","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Il Redentore","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Il_Redentore","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Il Redentore is a Palladian church on the Giudecca in Venice. Its temple-front vocabulary belongs to the Venetian late Renaissance.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-232","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-232","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which spatial arrangement is characteristic of the basilica complex shown?","value":"Two superimposed churches, conventionally called the Upper and Lower Basilicas","group":"Assisi: stacked basilicas and pilgrimage","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Basilica of Saint Francis — The Basilica","verificationUrl":"https://basilica.sanfrancesco.org/en/explore-the-basilica/the-basilica","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Assisi","key":"Assisi","title":"Basilica of Saint Francis — The Basilica","url":"https://basilica.sanfrancesco.org/en/explore-the-basilica/the-basilica","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read_partial","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"The official site distinguishes the Upper Church, Lower Church and tomb. The superimposed spatial arrangement was also checked against the supplied image and inherited architectural record.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn351418view0","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"The Basilica of Saint Francis at Assisi has superimposed upper and lower churches. Its form serves worship and pilgrimage associated with Saint Francis, while its painting cycles connect it with the development of Italian art.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-233","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-233","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Louis XIV's development of Versailles is closely associated with which political system?","value":"Absolute monarchy","group":"Versailles political function","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Palace and Park of Versailles","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/83/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Versailles","key":"Versailles","title":"UNESCO — Palace and Park of Versailles","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/83/","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Versailles linked royal residence, court ceremony and political authority. Its scale and ordered landscape made royal power visible.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-237","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-237","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which terms name related turn-of-the-twentieth-century design movements?","value":"Art Nouveau — Jugendstil — Modernisme","group":"Art Nouveau naming across Europe","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Art Nouveau","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Art_Nouveau","key":"Art_Nouveau","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Art Nouveau","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Art Nouveau had regional names and variants, including Jugendstil in German-speaking contexts and Modernisme in Catalonia. The terms do not imply identical work everywhere.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-238","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-238","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pair of architects is correctly linked with a major city of practice?","value":"Victor Horta — Brussels; Otto Wagner — Vienna","group":"Horta and Hoffmann different cities","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Major Town Houses of Victor Horta","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1005/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Horta","key":"Horta","title":"UNESCO — Major Town Houses of Victor Horta","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1005/","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Otto Wagner","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_Wagner","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Wagner","key":"Wagner","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Otto Wagner","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_Wagner","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Horta's Brussels houses and Wagner's Viennese work belong to related modernizing currents around 1900, but to different urban settings.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-239","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-239","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which combination best locates Antoni Gaudí's work?","value":"Barcelona — Catalan Modernisme — around 1900","group":"Gaudi and Catalan Modernisme","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Works of Antoni Gaudí","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Gaudi","key":"Gaudi","title":"UNESCO — Works of Antoni Gaudí","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/320/","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Gaudí's career belongs to the Catalan cultural and architectural context of the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-242","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-242","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Who succeeded Hannes Meyer as director of the Bauhaus?","value":"Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","group":"Bauhaus school directors","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Bauhaus","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bauhaus","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Bauhaus_directors","key":"Bauhaus_directors","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Bauhaus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bauhaus","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Mies van der Rohe succeeded Meyer in 1930. Gropius was the founding director, followed by Meyer and then Mies.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-243","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-243","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Mies van der Rohe's Barcelona Pavilion was designed as what?","value":"Germany's pavilion for the 1929 international exposition","group":"Barcelona Pavilion and modern classicism","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Barcelona Pavilion","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona_Pavilion","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Barcelona_Pavilion","key":"Barcelona_Pavilion","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Barcelona Pavilion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona_Pavilion","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The pavilion was an exhibition building. Its original function helps explain its open spatial composition rather than a conventional sequence of domestic rooms.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-247","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-247","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which feature is central to Brasília's modernist Pilot Plan?","value":"A monumental governmental axis combined with planned residential sectors","group":"Brasília: plan and urban functions","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Brasilia","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/445/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Brasilia","key":"Brasilia","title":"UNESCO — Brasilia","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/445/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Costa’s Pilot Plan, monumental and transport axes, functional sectors and superblocks; Niemeyer’s principal public buildings.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn467174view4","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"Lúcio Costa organized Brasília around intersecting axes, a monumental governmental zone and planned residential superblocks. The plan is a modernist organization of urban functions, not the gradual extension of a medieval core.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-249","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-249","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which engineer worked with Jørn Utzon on the Sydney Opera House?","value":"Ove Arup","group":"Sydney Opera House collaboration","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Sydney Opera House","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/166/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Sydney","key":"Sydney","title":"UNESCO — Sydney Opera House","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/166/","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Arup and his team collaborated with Utzon on the structural engineering of the opera house.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-254","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-254","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which architect wrote Complexity and Contradiction in Architecture?","value":"Robert Venturi","group":"Venturi complexity","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Complexity and Contradiction in Architecture","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complexity_and_Contradiction_in_Architecture","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Venturi","key":"Venturi","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Complexity and Contradiction in Architecture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complexity_and_Contradiction_in_Architecture","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Venturi's 1966 book challenged simplified modernist formulas and argued for richer, sometimes contradictory architectural meanings.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-256","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-256","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which building–architect association is incorrect?","value":"Sydney Opera House — Norman Foster","group":"High tech authorship discrimination","major":"Architecture","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Sydney Opera House","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/166/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Sydney","key":"Sydney","title":"UNESCO — Sydney Opera House","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/166/","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Lloyd's building","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lloyd%27s_building","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Lloyds","key":"Lloyds","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Lloyd's building","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lloyd%27s_building","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Centre Pompidou","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centre_Pompidou","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Pompidou","key":"Pompidou","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Centre Pompidou","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centre_Pompidou","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Guggenheim Museum Bilbao","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guggenheim_Museum_Bilbao","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Bilbao","key":"Bilbao","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Guggenheim Museum Bilbao","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guggenheim_Museum_Bilbao","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — HSBC Main Building, Hong Kong","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HSBC_Main_Building,_Hong_Kong","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-HSBC","key":"HSBC","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — HSBC Main Building, Hong Kong","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HSBC_Main_Building,_Hong_Kong","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The Sydney Opera House is associated with Jørn Utzon, not Foster. The remaining pairs belong to late-twentieth-century architecture.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-277","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-277","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which artistic concern is most closely associated with the painting shown?","value":"The dignity and hardship of ordinary rural labour","group":"Gleaners and Realism","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Gleaners","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gleaners","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Gleaners","key":"Gleaners","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Gleaners","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gleaners","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Millet's Gleaners gives prominence to poor rural workers. The subject belongs to the nineteenth-century realist interest in everyday social life.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-280","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-280","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The distorted form across the foreground becomes recognizable from an oblique viewpoint. What device is this?","value":"Anamorphosis","group":"Holbein anamorphosis","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"National Gallery — Holbein, The Ambassadors","verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/hans-holbein-the-younger-the-ambassadors","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Ambassadors","key":"Ambassadors","title":"National Gallery — Holbein, The Ambassadors","url":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/hans-holbein-the-younger-the-ambassadors","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"1533; Holbein; a distorted skull becomes legible from a particular angle (anamorphosis).","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn351418view2","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"The skull in Holbein's Ambassadors is anamorphic: its distortion is resolved from a particular viewing position. It also introduces a reminder of mortality.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-281","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-281","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which painter and royal court belong with the painting shown?","value":"Diego Velázquez — the Spanish Habsburg court","group":"Las Meninas court context","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Las Meninas","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Meninas","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Las_Meninas","key":"Las_Meninas","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Las Meninas","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Meninas","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Las Meninas was painted by Velázquez in the court of Philip IV of Spain. The painter's presence makes the making and viewing of the image part of its subject.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-282","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-282","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Despite its dramatic movement, the painting shown belongs to which established type?","value":"A civic militia group portrait","group":"Night Watch portrait commission","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Rijksmuseum — The Night Watch","verificationUrl":"https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/SK-C-5","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Night_Watch","key":"Night_Watch","title":"Rijksmuseum — The Night Watch","url":"https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/SK-C-5","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Rembrandt, 1642; Amsterdam civic militia company, not a literal night battle.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn351418view3","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"Rembrandt's Night Watch is a commissioned group portrait of an Amsterdam civic guard company, not a literal painting of a night-time battle.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-284","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-284","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which description correctly identifies the format and subject of the work shown?","value":"A tondo showing the Holy Family","group":"Doni Tondo format and subject","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Uffizi — Holy Family, known as the Doni Tondo","verificationUrl":"https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/holy-family-known-as-the-doni-tondo","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Doni","key":"Doni","title":"Uffizi — Holy Family, known as the Doni Tondo","url":"https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/holy-family-known-as-the-doni-tondo","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_search_excerpt_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Michelangelo; round panel of the Holy Family, dated 1505–1506.","retrieval_reference":"turn574826search4","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"Michelangelo's Doni Tondo is a circular painting of Mary, Joseph and the infant Christ. Tondo describes the format, not the painting technique.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-285","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-285","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which intellectual context helps explain the classical figures in the painting shown?","value":"Renaissance engagement with ancient mythology","group":"Primavera Renaissance mythology","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Uffizi — Botticelli, Spring","verificationUrl":"https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/botticelli-spring","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Primavera","key":"Primavera","title":"Uffizi — Botticelli, Spring","url":"https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/botticelli-spring","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Botticelli; c.1480 in this catalogue; mythological figures including Venus, Cupid, Mercury and the Graces. The catalogue warns that the full programme remains debated.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn351418view4","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"Botticelli's Primavera places classical mythological figures in a Renaissance painting. Recognizing that cultural setting is more important here than memorizing every possible interpretation.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-286","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-286","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which feature is particularly characteristic of the Van Gogh landscape shown?","value":"Directional brushwork","group":"Van Gogh expressive landscape","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Van Gogh Museum — Wheatfield with Crows","verificationUrl":"https://www.vangoghmuseum.nl/en/collection/s0149V1962","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Wheatfield","key":"Wheatfield","title":"Van Gogh Museum — Wheatfield with Crows","url":"https://www.vangoghmuseum.nl/en/collection/s0149V1962","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Strongly directed strokes give the landscape movement and emotional intensity.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-292","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-292","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"How does the David shown differ from Michelangelo's famous Florentine David?","value":"This is Donatello's bronze; Michelangelo's is marble","group":"Donatello and Michelangelo Davids","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Donatello)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Donatello)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Donatello_David","key":"Donatello_David","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Donatello)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Donatello)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Michelangelo)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Michelangelo)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Michelangelo_David","key":"Michelangelo_David","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Michelangelo)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Michelangelo)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Donatello's youthful bronze David predates Michelangelo's larger marble figure. Subject, artist, material and date are separate facts that must be connected.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-293","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-293","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which association fits the sculpture shown?","value":"Antonio Canova — Neoclassicism — classical mythology","group":"Canova and classical revival sculpture","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Psyche Revived by Cupid's Kiss","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psyche_Revived_by_Cupid%27s_Kiss","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Canova_Psyche","key":"Canova_Psyche","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Psyche Revived by Cupid's Kiss","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psyche_Revived_by_Cupid%27s_Kiss","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Canova's Psyche Revived by Cupid's Kiss adapts a classical myth in a late-eighteenth-century sculptural language. It is not an ancient Greek original.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-299","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-299","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which season and artistic setting belong with the painting shown?","value":"Winter — sixteenth-century Netherlandish painting","group":"Bruegel seasons and landscape","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Hunters in the Snow","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hunters_in_the_Snow","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Hunters","key":"Hunters","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Hunters in the Snow","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hunters_in_the_Snow","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Bruegel's Hunters in the Snow belongs to a cycle of seasonal landscapes. The scene integrates human work and leisure into a broad winter environment.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-300","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-300","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which social and historical setting best fits the painting shown?","value":"A peasant celebration — sixteenth-century Netherlands","group":"Bruegel Peasant Wedding: society and period","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Peasant Wedding","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Peasant_Wedding","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Peasant_Wedding","key":"Peasant_Wedding","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Peasant Wedding","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Peasant_Wedding","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Bruegel's Peasant Wedding depicts a rural wedding celebration in the sixteenth-century Netherlandish world. Food, service and social grouping—not a mythological hero or royal portrait—organize the scene.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-301","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-301","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Fragonard’s The Swing is associated with:","value":"Rococo","group":"Rococo and patronage","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Swing (Fragonard)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Swing_(Fragonard)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Swing","key":"Swing","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — The Swing (Fragonard)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Swing_(Fragonard)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Its playful aristocratic subject and decorative handling are characteristic of Rococo painting.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-302","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-302","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The enthroned ruler in this portrait by Ingres is:","value":"Napoleon I","group":"Ingres Napoleon imperial imagery","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Napoleon I on His Imperial Throne","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon_I_on_His_Imperial_Throne","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Napoleon_Ingres","key":"Napoleon_Ingres","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Napoleon I on His Imperial Throne","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon_I_on_His_Imperial_Throne","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Ingres presents Napoleon with ceremonial objects and symbols of imperial rule.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-306","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-306","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"The emergence of Dada in Zurich is closely linked to which historical setting?","value":"The First World War","group":"Dada and wartime dissent","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Dada","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dada","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Dada","key":"Dada","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Dada","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dada","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Dada emerged during the First World War and challenged conventional cultural values and artistic authority. Its chronology is distinct from Surrealism's later formal organization.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-307","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-307","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which association is conventional in accounts of Cubism?","value":"Analytic Cubism — fragmented analysis; Synthetic Cubism — constructed forms and collage","group":"Cubist collage and phase","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Cubism","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubism","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Cubism","key":"Cubism","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Cubism","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubism","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The labels distinguish tendencies within Cubism. Collage and papiers collés are especially associated with the Synthetic phase, though the boundary is not absolute.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-308","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-308","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which association correctly distinguishes two sculptures of David?","value":"Michelangelo — poised marble figure; Bernini — twisting figure in action","group":"Renaissance versus Baroque sculpture","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Michelangelo)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Michelangelo)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Michelangelo_David","key":"Michelangelo_David","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Michelangelo)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Michelangelo)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Bernini)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Bernini)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Bernini_David","key":"Bernini_David","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Bernini)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Bernini)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Michelangelo's David presents a poised figure, while Bernini's later David twists into the action of throwing. The same subject can express different period styles.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-309","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-309","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which subject comes from classical mythology rather than the Bible?","value":"The judgment of Paris","group":"Christian and classical subjects","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Judgement of Paris","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judgement_of_Paris","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Judgment_Paris","key":"Judgment_Paris","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Judgement of Paris","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judgement_of_Paris","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Paris's judgment concerns the goddesses Hera, Athena and Aphrodite. The other subjects belong to biblical narratives frequently represented in European art.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-311","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-311","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which pairing distinguishes relief from intaglio printing?","value":"Woodcut — raised surface prints; engraving — recessed lines print","group":"Printmaking processes contrast","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Printmaking","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printmaking","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Printmaking","key":"Printmaking","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Printmaking","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printmaking","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"A woodcut is a relief process. Engraving is intaglio, where ink in incised lines transfers to paper. The words describe processes, not just visual styles.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-312","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-312","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which pair correctly associates a movement with an important early centre?","value":"Fauvism — Paris; De Stijl — the Netherlands","group":"Art movement geography","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fauvism","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fauvism","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Fauvism","key":"Fauvism","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fauvism","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fauvism","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — De Stijl","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Stijl","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-De_Stijl","key":"De_Stijl","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — De Stijl","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Stijl","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Fauvism emerged in the Parisian context of the early 1900s. De Stijl was organized in the Netherlands around 1917 and pursued a different abstract vocabulary.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-313","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-313","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which artist and movement are associated with Spiral Jetty?","value":"Robert Smithson — Land Art","group":"Land Art and site","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Spiral Jetty","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiral_Jetty","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Spiral_Jetty","key":"Spiral_Jetty","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Spiral Jetty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiral_Jetty","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Spiral Jetty is Robert Smithson's earthwork in Utah's Great Salt Lake. The other artists also worked with landscape or large-scale interventions, so knowing the movement alone does not identify this work.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-314","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-314","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which pairing identifies two different image-making processes?","value":"Daguerreotype — early photography; lithography — planographic printing","group":"Photography and painting medium distinction","major":"Art History","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Daguerreotype","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daguerreotype","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Daguerreotype","key":"Daguerreotype","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Daguerreotype","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daguerreotype","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Lithography","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithography","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Lithography","key":"Lithography","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Lithography","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithography","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"A daguerreotype is a photographic image on a prepared metal plate. Lithography prints from a surface whose image and non-image areas differ chemically, not by relief depth.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-328","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-328","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Why was the Rosetta Stone important for deciphering Egyptian hieroglyphs?","value":"It presented the same decree in different scripts, including Greek","group":"Rosetta Stone and decipherment","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Rosetta Stone","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_Stone","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Rosetta","key":"Rosetta","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Rosetta Stone","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_Stone","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The stone carries a decree in hieroglyphic, Demotic and Greek scripts. The readable Greek text provided a route into the Egyptian versions; it was not a ready-made dictionary.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-329","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-329","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The stele of Hammurabi is chiefly associated with:","value":"Law","group":"Hammurabi and state authority","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Code of Hammurabi","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_of_Hammurabi","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Hammurabi","key":"Hammurabi","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Code of Hammurabi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_of_Hammurabi","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Its inscriptions record legal judgments associated with the Babylonian king.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-336","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-336","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Charlemagne's coronation as emperor in 800 linked his rule most directly with which institution?","value":"The papacy in Rome","group":"Charlemagne and imperial coronation","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Charlemagne","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlemagne","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Charlemagne","key":"Charlemagne","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Charlemagne","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlemagne","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne in Rome. The event linked a Frankish ruler to a renewed western imperial title and to the political role of the papacy.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-340","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-340","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Hundred Years' War principally involved the crowns of which countries?","value":"England and France","group":"Hundred Years War dynastic conflict","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Hundred Years' War","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hundred_Years%27_War","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Hundred_Years","key":"Hundred_Years","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Hundred Years' War","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hundred_Years%27_War","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The conflict concerned claims to the French crown and control of territory. Its conventional dates, 1337–1453, span more than a hundred years and include long interruptions.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-341","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-341","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"After the Ottoman conquest of 1453, Constantinople became what?","value":"The capital of the Ottoman Empire","group":"Ottoman and Byzantine capitals","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fall of Constantinople","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_Constantinople","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Constantinople_1453","key":"Constantinople_1453","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fall of Constantinople","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_Constantinople","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The conquest ended the Byzantine Empire and made the city an Ottoman imperial capital. Its earlier Byzantine monuments then acquired new uses and additions.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-342","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-342","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which navigator is correctly matched with a voyage?","value":"Vasco da Gama — Europe to India around southern Africa","group":"Exploration routes","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Vasco da Gama","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasco_da_Gama","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Da_Gama","key":"Da_Gama","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Vasco da Gama","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasco_da_Gama","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Da Gama reached India by sea in 1498 after sailing around southern Africa. The route connected European and Indian Ocean trading worlds without a canal through Egypt.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-352","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-352","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which statesman and kingdom were central to German unification in 1871?","value":"Otto von Bismarck — Prussia","group":"German unification and Prussia","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Unification of Germany","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unification_of_Germany","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-German_unification","key":"German_unification","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Unification of Germany","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unification_of_Germany","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Bismarck was the leading Prussian statesman in the process that culminated in the German Empire in 1871. This was distinct from the Italian unification associated with Cavour and Garibaldi.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-355","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-355","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which country belonged to the Central Powers during the First World War?","value":"The Ottoman Empire","group":"First World War alliance context","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Triple Entente","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triple_Entente","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-WWI_Alliances","key":"WWI_Alliances","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Triple Entente","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triple_Entente","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The Ottoman Empire fought alongside Germany and Austria-Hungary. France and Britain belonged to the opposing coalition, while Japan and later the United States also fought against the Central Powers.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-357","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-357","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Franklin D. Roosevelt's New Deal was a response to which crisis?","value":"The Great Depression","group":"New Deal policy response","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — New Deal","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Deal","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-New_Deal","key":"New_Deal","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — New Deal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Deal","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The New Deal introduced relief, recovery and reform measures in the 1930s. Its context is the Great Depression, not the later Cold War or energy crises.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-359","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-359","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Munich Agreement of 1938 permitted Germany to annex which territory?","value":"The Sudetenland in Czechoslovakia","group":"Munich Agreement and appeasement","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Munich Agreement","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munich_Agreement","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Munich","key":"Munich","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Munich Agreement","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munich_Agreement","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Munich concerned the Sudetenland and is associated with the policy of appeasement. It preceded the German invasion of Poland that began the European war in 1939.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-372","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-372","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which association between a leader and a country is correct?","value":"Kwame Nkrumah — Ghana","group":"Decolonization leaders and countries","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — History of Ghana","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Ghana","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Ghana_Independence","key":"Ghana_Independence","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — History of Ghana","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Ghana","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Nkrumah is associated with Ghana's independence in 1957. The other names also belong to twentieth-century political history but are paired with the wrong countries here.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-373","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-373","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which country granted women the right to vote in national parliamentary elections in 1893?","value":"New Zealand","group":"Women's suffrage milestones","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Women's suffrage in New Zealand","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women%27s_suffrage_in_New_Zealand","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-NZ_Suffrage","key":"NZ_Suffrage","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Women's suffrage in New Zealand","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women%27s_suffrage_in_New_Zealand","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"New Zealand's 1893 legislation is a major milestone in women's suffrage. Voting rights and the right to stand for office did not necessarily arrive together.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-374","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-374","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which text belongs to the French Revolution rather than the aftermath of the Second World War?","value":"Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen","group":"Rights declarations and eras","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Declaration_of_the_Rights_of_Man_and_of_the_Citizen","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-French_Rights","key":"French_Rights","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Declaration_of_the_Rights_of_Man_and_of_the_Citizen","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Universal Declaration of Human Rights","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Declaration_of_Human_Rights","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-UDHR","key":"UDHR","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Universal Declaration of Human Rights","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Declaration_of_Human_Rights","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_note":"403 response; no body retrieved.","verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The French declaration dates to 1789. The Universal Declaration dates to 1948, so their related language of rights should not erase their different historical settings.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-388","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-388","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which list contains only permanent members of the UN Security Council?","value":"China, France, Russia, United Kingdom, United States","group":"Security Council seats","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UN Security Council — Current Members","verificationUrl":"https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/content/current-members","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-UN_Council","key":"UN_Council","title":"UN Security Council — Current Members","url":"https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/content/current-members","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_search_excerpt_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Names of the five permanent members. Only this institutional composition is used, not the rotating members.","retrieval_reference":"turn420499search16","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"The five permanent seats are held by China, France, Russia, the United Kingdom and the United States. Economic size or population does not automatically confer permanent membership.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-389","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-389","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which institution prosecutes individuals for crimes such as genocide under its founding statute?","value":"The International Criminal Court","group":"International criminal jurisdiction","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — International Criminal Court","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-ICC","key":"ICC","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — International Criminal Court","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Criminal_Court","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_note":"403 response; no body retrieved.","verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"UN Information Service Vienna — The United Nations in general","verificationUrl":"https://unis.unvienna.org/unis/en/topics/the-un-in-general.html","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-ICJ","key":"ICJ","title":"UN Information Service Vienna — The United Nations in general","url":"https://unis.unvienna.org/unis/en/topics/the-un-in-general.html","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_search_excerpt_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"International Court of Justice handles legal disputes submitted by states and advisory opinions.","retrieval_reference":"turn420499search20","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"The ICC concerns individual criminal responsibility within its jurisdiction. The ICJ, by contrast, principally settles legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-391","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-391","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The World Trade Organization is primarily concerned with which activity?","value":"Rules governing international trade","group":"World Trade Organization framework","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"World Trade Organization — What is the WTO?","verificationUrl":"https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/whatis_e/whatis_e.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-WTO","key":"WTO","title":"World Trade Organization — What is the WTO?","url":"https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/whatis_e/whatis_e.htm","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The WTO provides a framework for trade agreements and the handling of trade disputes. It is not a world government or a global central bank.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-393","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-393","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which organization has a central mandate to protect refugees?","value":"UNHCR","group":"Refugees and migration institutions","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNHCR — About UNHCR","verificationUrl":"https://www.unhcr.org/about-unhcr","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-UNHCR","key":"UNHCR","title":"UNHCR — About UNHCR","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/about-unhcr","source_type":"museum_or_institutional_reference","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_note":"429 response; no body retrieved.","verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"UNHCR is the UN Refugee Agency. Refugee protection includes concerns such as safety, legal status and durable solutions, not simply the movement of all tourists or workers.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-394","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-394","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which organization is associated with nuclear safeguards and the peaceful use of atomic energy?","value":"The International Atomic Energy Agency","group":"Atomic energy and safeguards","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"International Atomic Energy Agency — Overview","verificationUrl":"https://www.iaea.org/about/overview","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-IAEA","key":"IAEA","title":"International Atomic Energy Agency — Overview","url":"https://www.iaea.org/about/overview","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Cooperation in safe and peaceful nuclear uses; connection with safeguards.","retrieval_reference":"turn120397view5","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}}],"explanation74":"The IAEA promotes peaceful nuclear applications and carries out safeguards activities. It is not a electricity supplier or an international military alliance.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-405","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-405","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which example belongs most clearly to civil society rather than the state apparatus?","value":"An independent neighbourhood association","group":"Civil society distinction","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Civil society","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_society","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Civil_society","key":"Civil_society","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Civil society","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_society","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Civil society includes voluntary organizations and associations outside the state's formal institutions. Such bodies can participate in public life without exercising governmental authority.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-418","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-418","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"A Lorenz curve represents the distribution of:","value":"Income or wealth","group":"Lorenz curve purpose","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Lorenz curve","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorenz_curve","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Inequality","key":"Inequality","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Lorenz curve","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorenz_curve","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"It plots cumulative shares of income or wealth against cumulative shares of the population.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-423","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-423","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which pair of mountain range and continent is correct?","value":"Atlas — Africa; Andes — South America","group":"Mountain ranges and continents","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Atlas Mountains","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlas_Mountains","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Atlas_Mountains","key":"Atlas_Mountains","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Atlas Mountains","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlas_Mountains","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Andes","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andes","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Andes","key":"Andes","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Andes","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andes","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The Atlas Mountains extend across north-western Africa. The Andes run along the western side of South America, linking physical geography with the settlement patterns of several countries.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-424","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-424","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which river is correctly paired with a city it crosses?","value":"Seine — Paris","group":"Rivers and historic cities","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Seine","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seine","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Seine","key":"Seine","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Seine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seine","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Geography of Europe","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Europe","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-European_rivers","key":"European_rivers","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Geography of Europe","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Europe","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"The Seine crosses Paris. The other pairs confuse major European rivers: the Thames is associated with London, the Tiber with Rome and the Arno with Florence.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-425","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-425","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which country has a coastline on both the Atlantic Ocean and the Mediterranean Sea?","value":"Morocco","group":"Seas and adjoining countries","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Geography of Morocco","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Morocco","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Morocco","key":"Morocco","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Geography of Morocco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Morocco","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Morocco lies at the north-western corner of Africa, with Atlantic and Mediterranean coasts. Its position is important to routes near the Strait of Gibraltar.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-426","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-426","anchor":"MAJOR:GEOGRAPHY-AND-CIVICS","subject":"Which of these countries has no coastline?","value":"Switzerland","group":"Landlocked states","major":"Geography & Civics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Geography of Switzerland","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Switzerland","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Switzerland","key":"Switzerland","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Geography of Switzerland","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Switzerland","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Switzerland is landlocked in central Europe. A country can have major lakes and navigable rivers without having a sea coast.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-449","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-449","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which author and movement are correctly associated?","value":"Émile Zola — Naturalism","group":"Literary movements and authors","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Émile Zola","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89mile_Zola","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Zola","key":"Zola","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Émile Zola","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89mile_Zola","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Zola is a major figure in nineteenth-century literary Naturalism, with its interest in heredity, environment and social conditions. Flaubert is more closely associated with Realism, Hugo with Romanticism and Mallarmé with Symbolism.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-450","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-450","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which author and historical setting belong with Madame Bovary?","value":"Gustave Flaubert — nineteenth-century France","group":"Madame Bovary author and form","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Madame Bovary","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madame_Bovary","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Flaubert","key":"Flaubert","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Madame Bovary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madame_Bovary","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Madame Bovary was published in 1857 and is associated with Flaubert's prose and the realist novel. It is not a play or an opera.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-451","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-451","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which novel is correctly paired with its author?","value":"Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoevsky","group":"Russian novels and authors","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Russian literature","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_literature","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Russian_literature","key":"Russian_literature","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Russian literature","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_literature","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Dostoevsky wrote Crime and Punishment. Tolstoy wrote War and Peace, Chekhov The Cherry Orchard, Gogol Dead Souls and Pushkin Eugene Onegin.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-454","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-454","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which of these Shakespeare plays is a comedy rather than a tragedy?","value":"A Midsummer Night's Dream","group":"Shakespeare genres","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Shakespeare's plays","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shakespeare%27s_plays","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Shakespeare_genres","key":"Shakespeare_genres","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Shakespeare's plays","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shakespeare%27s_plays","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"A Midsummer Night's Dream is conventionally classed among Shakespeare's comedies. The other four titles are tragedies, despite their occasional comic scenes.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-458","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-458","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which work by Luigi Pirandello questions the relationship between characters and theatrical reality?","value":"Six Characters in Search of an Author","group":"Pirandello theatre and identity","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Six Characters in Search of an Author","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Six_Characters_in_Search_of_an_Author","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Pirandello","key":"Pirandello","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Six Characters in Search of an Author","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Six_Characters_in_Search_of_an_Author","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Pirandello's play brings unfinished characters into a theatre rehearsal. Its self-reflexive structure questions authorship, identity and the boundaries between performance and life.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-461","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-461","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which sequence of composers follows their broad historical periods?","value":"Bach — Baroque; Mozart — Classical; Chopin — Romantic","group":"Composers and periods","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — History of classical music","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_classical_music","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Composer_periods","key":"Composer_periods","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — History of classical music","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_classical_music","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Bach is central to the Baroque, Mozart to the Classical period and Chopin to Romanticism. Musical period labels do not map mechanically onto every visual-art style of the same date.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-462","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-462","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which opera was composed by Giuseppe Verdi?","value":"La traviata","group":"Italian opera composers","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Opera","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opera","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Opera_pairs","key":"Opera_pairs","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Opera","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opera","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Verdi’s opera adapts the story of Dumas fils’s La Dame aux camélias.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-463","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-463","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which composer is associated with Swan Lake and The Nutcracker?","value":"Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky","group":"Ballet and composer","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyotr_Ilyich_Tchaikovsky","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Tchaikovsky","key":"Tchaikovsky","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyotr_Ilyich_Tchaikovsky","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Tchaikovsky composed both ballet scores in the nineteenth century. Ballet links music with choreographed performance and differs from an opera built around sung drama.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-466","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-466","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which composer is especially associated with musical Impressionism?","value":"Claude Debussy","group":"Impressionist music and composer","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Claude Debussy","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Debussy","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Debussy","key":"Debussy","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Claude Debussy","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Debussy","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Debussy is frequently associated with the label, although his relationship to it was not straightforward. The term connects musical colour and atmosphere with a broader cultural discussion, not a literal painting method.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-467","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-467","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which director-performer is associated with The Kid, City Lights and Modern Times?","value":"Charlie Chaplin","group":"Silent cinema and visual storytelling","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Charlie Chaplin","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlie_Chaplin","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Chaplin","key":"Chaplin","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Charlie Chaplin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlie_Chaplin","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Chaplin created all three films. Modern Times, released in the sound era, deliberately retained much of the visual language associated with his silent-screen character.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-468","nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-468","anchor":"MAJOR:LANGUAGE-AND-LITERATURE","subject":"Which film was directed by Federico Fellini?","value":"La dolce vita","group":"Italian cinema director film","major":"Language & Literature","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — La dolce vita","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_dolce_vita","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Fellini","key":"Fellini","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — La dolce vita","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_dolce_vita","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"explanation74":"Fellini’s film follows a journalist through the social life of Rome.","previewOnly95":false,"review100":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0001","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0001","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which email field normally makes a copied recipient's address visible to the other recipients?","value":"Cc","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":431,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Cc recipients are visible; Bcc conceals the copied recipients' addresses.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0002","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0002","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"In public-key cryptography, which key must its owner keep secret?","value":"Private key","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":156,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"A public key can be shared; the corresponding private key must remain confidential.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0003","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0003","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"A program repeatedly executes instructions while a condition holds. This control structure is a:","value":"Loop","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":104,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"A loop repeats instructions; a variable stores a value that can change.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0004","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0004","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which computer component normally loses its working contents when power is removed?","value":"RAM","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":102,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Ordinary RAM is volatile memory, unlike storage intended to retain data without power.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0005","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0005","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which unit is commonly used to express processor clock frequency?","value":"Gigahertz","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":283,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Hertz measures cycles per second. Storage capacity is measured in bytes.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0006","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0006","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"In a relational database, a primary key serves principally to:","value":"Identify each record uniquely","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":776,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"A primary key distinguishes records and must not duplicate another record's key value.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0007","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0007","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which service translates an internet domain name into an IP address?","value":"DNS","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":559,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"The Domain Name System resolves names such as website domains to network addresses.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0008","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0008","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which term measures the delay in a network connection rather than its data-carrying capacity?","value":"Latency","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":575,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Latency measures delay; bandwidth describes a connection’s data-carrying capacity.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0009","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0009","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which file format is designed primarily for exchanging documents while preserving their page layout?","value":"PDF","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":512,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"PDF preserves document layout; CSV stores tabular text and MP3 stores audio.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0010","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0010","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"A fraudulent email impersonates a bank and requests a password. This is:","value":"Phishing","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":156,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Phishing uses impersonation to trick people into revealing information or taking unsafe actions.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0012","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0012","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which type of software translates source code into another executable form?","value":"Compiler","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":104,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"A compiler translates a program; a browser retrieves and presents web content.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0013","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0013","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which SQL instruction retrieves records from a database table?","value":"SELECT","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":776,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"SELECT reads data. INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE change stored records.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0014","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0014","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which technology normally connects nearby wireless headphones and a smartphone?","value":"Bluetooth","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":575,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Bluetooth connects nearby devices by radio, for example headphones and phones.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0016","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0016","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Which statement about the equinoxes is correct?","value":"Day and night have approximately equal lengths","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":328,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"At an equinox the Sun crosses the celestial equator; daylight and darkness are approximately equal.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0017","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0017","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Quartz is composed chiefly of:","value":"Silicon dioxide","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":529,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Quartz is silicon dioxide; calcite is calcium carbonate and halite is sodium chloride.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0018","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0018","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"Two species independently develop similar adaptations to similar environments. This is:","value":"Convergent evolution","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":393,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Convergent evolution produces similar traits in lineages without recent shared ancestry for that trait.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0019","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0019","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"A Mach number compares an object's speed with:","value":"The local speed of sound","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":215,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Mach 1 corresponds to the local speed of sound, which varies with conditions.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0020","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0020","anchor":"MAJOR:SCIENCE-AND-TECH","subject":"The median of a data set is determined by:","value":"The middle position after ordering","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"Science & Tech","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":782,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"The median is positional; the mean is the sum divided by the number of observations.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0022","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0022","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which Roman deity was associated especially with the sea?","value":"Neptune","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":420,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Neptune corresponds broadly to Greek Poseidon; Mars was associated with war.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0023","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0023","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The denarius was a form of Roman:","value":"Coinage","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":307,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"The denarius was an important Roman coin, originally struck in silver.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0024","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0024","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which crop reached Europe from the Americas after Columbus's voyages?","value":"Potato","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":169,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"The potato originated in the Americas; the other crops were cultivated in the ancient Mediterranean.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0025","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0025","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Huguenots were French followers of:","value":"Calvinist Protestantism","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":453,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Huguenot communities were part of the French Reformed Protestant tradition.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0026","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0026","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which dynasty ruled Russia immediately before the monarchy fell in 1917?","value":"Romanov","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":212,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"Nicholas II belonged to the Romanov dynasty, which had ruled Russia since the seventeenth century.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0027","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0027","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Enabling Act of 1933 primarily allowed Hitler’s government to:","value":"Legislate without normal parliamentary approval","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":90,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"The act dismantled parliamentary constraints and helped establish the Nazi dictatorship.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0028","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0028","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Which political system followed the Iranian monarchy after the 1979 revolution?","value":"Islamic republic","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":619,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"The shah's overthrow was followed by an Islamic republic led initially by Ayatollah Khomeini.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0029","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0029","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Habeas corpus is principally a safeguard against:","value":"Unlawful detention","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuestion":317,"supportScope":"Topic/format lineage only; not evidence for the new factual claim"}],"explanation74":"It provides a means to challenge the legality of detention before a court.","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0030","nodeId":"REVIEW98-ARCHED-NEW98-0030","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"The Etruscan civilization flourished chiefly in which part of the Italian peninsula?","value":"Central Italy","group":"Alpha coverage extension","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"AlphaTest source-concept lineage","sourceQuest